\paragraph{Main results and work structure.}  

To systematically investigate confirmation bias during the particle-picking stage, we examine the bias in the two leading approaches: classical template matching and deep learning-based methods. For template matching, we develop a theoretical framework and validate it through numerical experiments. While a comparable theory for deep learning methods is currently unavailable, we conduct extensive numerical experiments using the widely adopted particle picker Topaz~\cite{bepler2019positive}. Our analysis demonstrates that both approaches introduce systematic biases, often resulting in reconstructions that reflect prior assumptions rather than faithfully representing the underlying data.

In Section~\ref{sec:model}, we develop a theoretical framework to analyze how confirmation bias arises in template-matching particle picking methods. To quantify this bias, we introduce a metric that measures the similarity between the extracted particles and the original templates, based on the outputs of two key downstream tasks: 2D classification (relevant to cryo-EM) and 3D reconstruction (relevant to cryo-ET). For the theoretical analysis, we focus on the scenario where the input to the particle-picking algorithm consists entirely of pure noise. Remarkably, under standard conditions, both the resulting 2D class averages (in cryo-EM) and the 3D reconstructions (in cryo-ET) converge toward the particle-picking templates, despite the absence of any real underlying signal in the data.

In Section~\ref{sec:empirical}, we complement our theoretical analysis with empirical evidence using a standard cryo-EM software pipeline. We evaluate the output of RELION~\cite{kimanius2021new}, and Topaz~\cite{bepler2019positive} when applied to synthetic datasets, including pure-noise and low-SNR micrographs. We find that even in the presence of real particles, low-SNR conditions can result in hybrid reconstructions that combine true structural features with artifacts resembling the templates. Furthermore, we demonstrate that neural network pickers, such as Topaz, while more challenging to analyze theoretically, also introduce reproducible structural artifacts when applied to noise, indicating that learned priors can bias the computational pipeline.

In Section~\ref{sec:discussion}, we discuss the broader implications of these findings for the structural biology community. We analyze the trade-offs involved in key parameter choices, particularly selection thresholds, and propose strategies for mitigating bias in the particle-picking stage. We compare the statistical characteristics of confirmation bias introduced during particle picking with those arising at later stages of the reconstruction pipeline, such as 2D classification and 3D reconstruction. Additionally, we outline potential directions for future research to extend this work.

\section{Theory}\label{sec:model}
This section introduces a simplified model to investigate confirmation bias in the particle-picking process, where 2D noisy particles (in cryo-EM) or 3D sub-tomograms (in cryo-ET) are identified and extracted. To gain analytical insights, we analyze a particle picker based on template matching (Algorithm \ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}). While this model does not fully capture the intricate mechanism of real-world particle-pickers, it offers key theoretical insights into the mechanisms by which confirmation bias can emerge at this early stage of the pipeline. In Section \ref{sec:empirical}, to enrich this analysis, we provide empirically grounded evidence that reveals further intricacies of the phenomenon.

For clarity, formal statements and proofs of the main theoretical results are deferred to the appendix. Informal formulations are provided throughout the main text to aid readability. Throughout the text, $\langle a, b \rangle$ represents the inner product between the vectors $a$ and $b$.

\subsection{The particle-picking process} \label{subsec:generationModel}
\paragraph{The simplified particle-picking model.} We consider a set of $N$ candidate particles, denoted by $\ppp{y_i}_{i=0}^{N-1} \in \mathbb{R}^d$, where $d$ is the particle dimension (e.g., $d = 36 \times 36$ for cryo-EM, or $d = 36 \times 36 \times 36$ in cryo-ET). These candidates are assumed to be statistically independent and extracted from disjoint, non-overlapping regions of the micrograph. 
The particle picker is modeled as a binary function $f: \mathbb{R}^d \to \{0, 1\}$, and outputs 1 for particles, and 0 for non-particles. We denote by $M \leq N$ the number of particles extracted by the particle picker.

\paragraph{Particle-picking based on template-matching.}
The \textit{template-matching particle-picker}, detailed in Algorithm~\ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}, operates by cross-correlating each candidate particle $\{y_i\}_{i=0}^{N-1}$ with $L$ normalized templates $\{x_\ell\}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$ \footnote{This normalization corresponds to energy normalization commonly used in cross-correlation filtering in cryo-EM. Templates are normalized to unit norm to ensure correlation scores are directly comparable across templates and observations, preventing scaling bias during template matching.}. The templates $\ppp{x_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$ are either 2D projections from different orientations of a common underlying 3D template volume $V_{\s{template}}$ (in the case of cryo-EM), or different rotations of the 3D template model, without projections (in the case of cryo-ET). An observation is classified as a particle if its correlation with at least one template exceeds a fixed threshold $T$. The extracted particles are stored in the set $\mathcal{A}$, where the number of extracted particles is $|\mathcal{A}| = M$. It is important to note that the extracted particles in the set $\mathcal{A}$ are \textit{unlabeled}, meaning that for any given particle in $\mathcal{A}$, the specific template with which it has the highest correlation is unknown.

\begin{algorithm}[] 
  \caption{\texttt{Template-Matching Particle Picker}}
  \label{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}
  \textbf{Input:} $L$ templates $\{x_\ell\}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$, $N$ candidate particles $\{y_i\}_{i=0}^{N-1}$, and a threshold $T$. \\
  \textbf{Output:} Set \(\mathcal{A}\) of selected particles.
  \begin{algorithmic}[1]
    \State Initialize \(\mathcal{A} \gets \emptyset\)
    \For{$i = 0$ to $N-1$}
      \If{$\underset{0 \leq \ell < L-1} \max \langle y_i, x_\ell \rangle \geq T$}
        \State Add $y_i$ to $\mathcal{A}$: \(\mathcal{A} \gets \mathcal{A} \cup \{y_i\}\)
      \EndIf
    \EndFor
    \State \Return $\mathcal{A}$
  \end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}


\paragraph{Main assumptions.} 
Before presenting the theoretical results, we state the main assumptions required for the analysis. 
For template-matching-based particle picking, we assume an underlying 3D structure denoted by 
$V_{\s{template}}$, which serves as the reference volume. In cryo-EM, this template is typically projected into 2D images across a range of orientations, while in cryo-ET, it is rotated directly in 3D. We assume that the input micrographs and tomograms are corrupted by additive white Gaussian noise, consistent with the classical Einstein from noise experiment~\cite{shatsky2009method}. 
While this is a simplification, as experimental cryo-EM and cryo-ET data often contain structured contaminants and exhibit weakly correlated noise, modeling the noise as white and Gaussian offers a clean theoretical baseline.
We note that similar artifacts to the Einstein from Noise effect have also been observed under more general noise models~\cite{balanov2024einstein}.


\subsection{Theoretical analysis} 
To analyze confirmation bias in particle picking, we model the candidate particles $\{y_i\}_{i=0}^{N-1}$ as white Gaussian independent and identically distributed (i.i.d) noise (excerpts of pure noise micrographs), i.e., $\{y_i\}_{i=0}^{N-1} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2 I_{d \times d})$, where $\mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2 I_{d \times d})$ denotes a multivariate Gaussian distribution with mean zero and a diagonal covariance matrix with variance $\sigma^2$.  

\paragraph{Confirmation bias analysis through downstream tasks.} 
To quantify the confirmation bias introduced during particle picking, we define a metric that compares the extracted particles to the original templates used in the template-matching process. We assess this bias through two downstream tasks: 2D classification (relevant for cryo-EM), where the extracted particles are grouped into classes based on structural similarity and then averaged to increase the SNR,  and 3D reconstruction (relevant for cryo-ET), where the extracted subtomograms are aligned and averaged to reconstruct a 3D structure.
In both cases, we evaluate how closely the resulting 2D class averages and 3D reconstructions resemble the initial templates used for picking.

\paragraph{2D classification.} 
The 2D classification step in cryo-EM is often modeled using a Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM), a probabilistic framework in which each particle image is assumed to be drawn from a mixture of Gaussian distributions \cite{reynolds2009gaussian}. In this setting, each component of the mixture corresponds to a distinct viewing direction of the underlying 3D structure. 

Formally, the extracted particles $z_0, z_1, \ldots, z_{M-1} \in \mathbb{R}^d$ are modeled as independent samples from the GMM:
\begin{align}
    z_0, z_1, \ldots, z_{M-1} \stackrel{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \sum_{\ell=0}^{L-1} w_\ell \cdot \mathcal{N}(\mu_\ell, \Sigma), \label{eqn:GMMmodelMain}
\end{align}
where $\mu_\ell$ represents the mean of the $\ell$-th Gaussian component (i.e., a 2D class average), $w_\ell$ its mixing weight (satsifying $w_\ell \geq 0$, $\sum_{\ell=0}^{L-1} w_\ell = 1$), and $\Sigma$ is a shared covariance matrix across components. 
In the cryo-EM context, each $\mu_\ell$ corresponds to a projection of the 3D structure from a particular viewing direction. For simplicity, we assume that the number of mixture components $L$ matches the number of templates used in particle picking, although we recognize that this correspondence may not hold in practice.

It is important to emphasize that the GMM assumed in \eqref{eqn:GMMmodelMain} does not accurately capture the true distribution of particles obtained via template-based particle picking (see Algorithm \ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}), which does not follow Gaussian statistics; that is, the statistical model is \textit{misspecified}. This mismatch between the assumed and actual data-generating processes must be carefully considered when analyzing the estimated class averages. A detailed discussion of this model misspecification is provided in Appendix \ref{sec:misspecifiedModels}.

We note that the 2D classification model described in~\eqref{eqn:GMMmodelMain} omits a common component of practical workflows: in-plane alignment, which is a standard step in cryo-EM 2D classification pipelines~\cite{kimanius2021new}. This procedure estimates the optimal in-plane rotation and translation of each particle image to align it with a class average. Although we exclude in-plane alignment from our theoretical model to preserve analytical tractability, we empirically investigate its effect in Section~\ref{sec:empirical} and show that it does not affect the core properties identified in the simplified model of~\eqref{eqn:GMMmodelMain}.

The goal of 2D classification is to estimate the component means $\{\mu_\ell\}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$ based on the observed data. A standard approach is maximum likelihood estimation (MLE) \cite{wald1949note,scheres2012relion}, yielding estimates $\{\hat{\mu}_\ell\}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$. This leads to a key question:
\begin{quote}
    \textit{To what extent do the estimated class averages $\{ \hat{\mu}_\ell \}$ resemble the original templates $\{ x_\ell \}$ used in particle picking?}
\end{quote}
Ideally, if the estimation process is unbiased, the reconstructed class averages should show no correlation with the templates. In particular, for input particles consisting purely of noise, the class averages should approach zero. Any systematic correlation observed would indicate bias introduced earlier in the pipeline, likely by the particle picking algorithm.


\paragraph{2D classification theoretical results.} 
In Appendix \ref{sec:2Dclass} and Theorem \ref{thm:classesCentersVersusTemplates}, we prove that the estimated centroids produced by the 2D classification process,  $\{\hat{\mu}_\ell\}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$, converge to the original templates $\{x_\ell\}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$ (up to a scaling factor), when the input candidate particles $\{y_i\}_{i=0}^{N-1}$ consist purely of white Gaussian noise and both the number of candidate particles and the threshold parameter $T$ are sufficiently large. The result is summarized informally below.

\begin{thm}[Informal, cryo-EM confirmation bias via 2D classification]
\label{thm:classesCentersVersusTemplatesInformal}
Let $\{x_\ell\}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$ be a set of templates, and let the candidate particles $\{y_i\}_{i=0}^{N-1}$ be drawn i.i.d. from the Gaussian distribution $\mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2 I_{d \times d})$. Let $\mathcal{A}$ denote the set of extracted particles selected by Algorithm~\ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching} using a threshold parameter $T$. Let $\{\hat{\mu}_\ell\}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$ be the maximum-likelihood estimates of the GMM defined in~\eqref{eqn:GMMmodelMain}, computed from the extracted particles $\mathcal{A}$. Then,
\begin{align}
    \lim_{N, T \to \infty} \frac{\hat{\mu}_\ell}{T} = x_\ell, \quad \ell = 0, \ldots, L - 1.
\end{align}
\end{thm}

This result reveals a pronounced bias introduced by template-matching-based particle picking: the estimated class centroids retain full correlation (Pearson cross-correlation (PCC) of 1) with the templates used in the particle-picking stage, even when the input data is pure noise. This is in stark contrast to the unbiased expectation that averaging pure noise yields zero as the number of samples grows.

Figure~\ref{fig:3}(a) illustrates the effect of a high threshold on the bias. At low threshold values, the centroids do not align with the original templates because the extracted particles are not correctly associated with their corresponding classes. However, as the threshold increases, the estimated centroids progressively align more closely with the original templates. This result reveals a trade-off in selecting the threshold value during the particle picking stage, which will be further discussed in Section \ref{sec:discussion}.

