\subsection{Particle picker based on Topaz neural network}
Deep learning-based methods have become a widely used approach for particle picking in cryo-EM and cryo-ET, with Topaz being one of the most prominent examples~\cite{bepler2019positive}. Unlike template-matching approaches, which introduce bias through direct similarity to predefined structures, deep neural networks (DNNs) introduce bias through more subtle mechanisms. These include the composition of the training dataset (which may reflect prior structural assumptions), architectural choices, and the learning dynamics of the training process itself. Although the mechanisms differ, such models can still exhibit confirmation bias by preferentially selecting particles that resemble features emphasized during training. In our experiments, we use the widely adopted Topaz network to investigate model bias in deep learning-based particle pickers and find that it also consistently produces structured outputs influenced by its training priors, albeit to a lesser extent than template matching.

To analyze this phenomenon, we used the pre-trained Topaz model provided by Bepler et al.~\cite{bepler2019positive} without performing any additional training. We worked with micrographs of size $2048 \times 2048$, which were down-sampled in Topaz by a factor of $s=2$, due to GPU memory constraints. During extraction, we set the radius to $r=9$ (i.e., original particle projection dimensions were presumed to be $d = 36 \times 36$) and the coordinates scaling factor to $x=2$ (to account for aforementioned down-sampling). 

Figure~\ref{fig:6}(a–c) illustrates the presence of confirmation bias in Topaz network (as a representative of deep learning-based particle pickers).
In Figure~\ref{fig:6}(a), the average of the particles extracted by Topaz reveals a faint circular structure at the center, which is an unexpected result under a truly unbiased system, where the mean should converge to zero. This artifact closely resembles the central pattern observed in Figure~\ref{fig:6}(b), which shows the mean of particles selected via template matching using a large and diverse set of templates. The similarity between these results suggests that both approaches introduce structured bias into the picking process. For comparison, Figure~\ref{fig:6}(c) shows the mean of randomly selected patches from pure noise, which converges to zero, serving as an unbiased reference. 

To quantify the impact of confirmation bias on 3D reconstruction, we conducted a controlled experiment using pure-noise micrographs that are split into two independent halves. Each half is processed using one of three particle-picking strategies: template matching, Topaz, or random selection. We then performed independent ab initio 3D reconstructions on each half using the RELION VDAM algorithm. Following standard practice, we assess the consistency between the resulting half-maps using Fourier Shell Correlation (FSC), as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:6}(d).

Both Topaz and template matching produce reconstructions with high FSC values between the halves, indicating that the selected particles give rise to consistent, method-dependent structures, even though the input data contain only noise.
In contrast, random selection yields near-zero FSC values, as expected under an unbiased model. Among the three methods, template matching exhibits the strongest bias, followed by Topaz, with random picking serving as a near-unbiased baseline. These results provide additional empirical evidence that the FSC gold standard can be misled by systematic errors, consistent with observations in~\cite{sorzano2022bias}.

