\section{Discussion and Outlook} \label{sec:discussion}

To contextualize the confirmation bias analyzed in this work, we compare it to other sources of bias that arise throughout the cryo-EM and cryo-ET reconstruction pipeline. We then discuss strategies for mitigating this bias and conclude with several promising directions for future research.

\subsection{Different statistical models for confirmation bias in cryo-EM}

Several stages of the cryo-EM and cryo-ET processing pipeline are vulnerable to confirmation bias, stemming from the use of initial templates or strong prior assumptions~\cite[Section 4.1]{sorzano2022bias}. 
 Key examples include:
(1) Particle picking, where 2D particles (in cryo-EM) or 3D subtomograms (in cryo-ET) are extracted using template-based or learning-based methods~\cite{scheres2015semi, heimowitz2018apple, bepler2019positive,eldar2020klt};
(2) 2D classification (cryo-EM), where particles are clustered and averaged;
(3) 3D reconstruction, including subtomogram averaging in cryo-ET, which is typically carried out using the expectation-maximization algorithm. As demonstrated and analyzed in~\cite{balanov2025expectation}, these algorithms are especially vulnerable to bias in low SNR conditions, where unverified assumptions can significantly shape the final reconstruction. 

Each of these stages introduces bias through different statistical mechanisms. Figure~\ref{fig:7} compares the forms of confirmation bias that occur during particle picking with those that emerge in downstream tasks, such as 2D classification. Below, we describe and contrast the Einstein from Noise phenomenon, which was presented in Section \ref{sec:intro} and the confirmation bias observed in 2D classification with the bias arising from particle picking.

\paragraph{The Einstein from Noise phenomenon.}  
Although frequently mentioned in the context of template-based particle picking, the Einstein from Noise effect arises from a distinct model. In the Einstein from Noise experiment, all observations are aligned and averaged with respect to a fixed template. In contrast, particle picking involves selecting a small subset of patches from a large micrograph, specifically, those with the highest cross-correlation scores relative to the template. This key distinction gives rise to different types of confirmation bias and artifacts. Particle picking tends to produce reconstructions that closely resemble the template, differing only by a global scale factor (Theorems \ref{thm:classesCentersVersusTemplatesInformal} and \ref{thm:3DstructureInformal}). In contrast, the Einstein from Noise effect produces an average that is positively correlated with the template but not identical~\cite{balanov2024einstein}. 

\paragraph{Confirmation bias in 2D classification and 3D volume reconstruction.}  
In later stages of cryo-EM processing, such as 2D classification and 3D volume estimation, iterative algorithms often use predefined templates for initialization. When applied to data with no true signal (e.g., white Gaussian noise), these methods may still produce output that resembles the initial templates, even though the input data provides no evidence for such structure~\cite{balanov2024confirmation}.

Figure~\ref{fig:7}(b) illustrates this phenomenon in the context of 2D classification. In this example, the input consisted entirely of pure noise, and the initial templates were set to $L=30$ distinct projections of the Beta-galactosidase structure, each of size $d=36 \times 36$. A total of $M=5 \times 10^5$ particles were processed using a 2D classification based on the expectation-maximization algorithm, run for 20 iterations. Even without a signal, the resulting class averages exhibit a positive correlation with the templates. However, unlike the case of template-based particle picking, the final class averages do not match the templates exactly (even up to a scale factor), though they still appear visually similar. 
It is worth noting that many modern 2D classification and 3D ab initio reconstruction algorithms now adopt reference-free initialization strategies (e.g., random initializations), which help mitigate such bias~\cite{kimanius2021new}. Nevertheless, when templates are introduced, whether explicitly or implicitly, similar confirmation effects can still occur.

\paragraph{Hallucinated heterogeneity in conformational analysis.}  
A distinct form of confirmation bias arises in methods for continuous heterogeneity analysis, particularly when state-of-the-art models are applied to structurally homogeneous datasets. In \cite{gilles2025cryo}, the authors benchmarked RECOVAR \cite{gilles2025cryo}, 3DFlex \cite{punjani20233dflex}, and cryoDRGN \cite{zhong2021cryodrgn} on a synthetic homogeneous dataset with high levels of noise. Despite the data consisting of a single conformation, all three methods showed signs of hallucinated heterogeneity. This demonstrates that both deep-learning methods (3DFlex and cryoDRGN) and classical statistical methods (RECOVAR) are not immune to this form of bias.


