\subsection{Strategies for mitigating confirmation bias in particle picking}

Previous studies have explored the detection and mitigation of various forms of bias in cryo-EM. For example, recent work~\cite{sorzano2022bias} has proposed several practical strategies to reduce reconstruction bias, including identifying bias at the level of estimated parameters, performing multiple independent reconstructions, averaging parameter estimates to reduce variance, applying phase randomization at the image level, and validating algorithms across diverse preprocessing and reconstruction settings.
Despite these advances, the particle-picking stage remains a particularly vulnerable and relatively understudied source of bias. Here, we focus specifically on computational strategies to mitigate bias during particle picking, incorporating both practical considerations and new theoretically grounded approaches.

\paragraph{Bypassing particle picking.}
A promising strategy is to eliminate the particle-picking step altogether. Recent methods for direct structure determination, such as those based on the method of moments~\cite{bendory2023toward} or  approximations to expectation-maximization~\cite{kreymer2023stochastic}, aim to reconstruct 3D volumes directly from raw micrographs, thereby avoiding any particle selection. These approaches offer a principled way to avoid selection bias 
entirely~\cite{balanov2025note}, though their current resolution and computational feasibility remain limited. 

\paragraph{Statistical control of selection thresholds.}
All particle-picking methods inevitably produce both false positives (non-particles mistakenly identified as particles) and false negatives (true particles that are missed). A common practice today is to tune picking parameters to favor high recall, that is, to select as many candidate particles as possible. This strategy aims to maximize the extraction of structural information from the micrographs~\cite{sorzano2022bias}. 

In particular, in template-matching based approaches (see Algorithm~\ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}), the selection threshold $T$ plays a crucial role in this process and has a significant impact on the extent of confirmation bias introduced during particle selection.
A high threshold offers certain advantages: (1) it reduces false positives by selecting only the strongest matches to the templates, increasing the likelihood that picked particles represent true biological structures; and (2) it streamlines downstream processing by limiting the number of particles.
However, this approach comes with trade-offs. First, as demonstrated in Section~\ref{sec:model}, a high threshold can amplify confirmation bias, skewing reconstructions toward the input templates even when the data consists of pure noise. Second, it reduces the number of selected particles, potentially weakening the statistical power of the reconstruction and limiting achievable resolution.

Despite its importance, the threshold $T$ is typically chosen heuristically or manually, without rigorous statistical justification. A more principled alternative is to set thresholds using multiple hypothesis testing frameworks that control the false discovery rate (FDR)\cite{benjamini1995controlling}. These techniques guarantee that the expected proportion of false positives remains below a predefined level, while typically achieving high statistical power. As a result, they help reduce the selection of spurious particles that arise due to random correlations with the templates. However, these methods are often specifically tailored to well-specified statistical models and may not generalize easily to more complex or poorly understood settings~\cite{eldar2024object}. Recent developments, such as knockoff-based procedures, offer a promising direction for extending FDR control to more flexible and model-agnostic scenarios by introducing synthetic variables that mimic the data's dependence structure~\cite{barber2015controlling}.

\paragraph{Template filtering and design.}
Several tools in current cryo-EM pipelines aim to reduce bias by preprocessing the templates themselves~\cite{scheres2012relion}. For example, low-pass filtering the templates reduces their high-frequency features, which are most susceptible to overfitting by noise. This encourages the selection of broader structural motifs rather than fine-grained details, mitigating high-frequency confirmation bias. Our theoretical model (see Section~\ref{sec:model}) shows that this practice leads to reconstructions that converge toward low-pass-filtered versions of the templates. While not a complete remedy, such filtering is routinely employed in practice and is supported by empirical evidence, including the analysis presented in~\cite{balanov2024einstein}.

\paragraph{Template-free picking and alternative similarity measures.}
In addition to template-based methods, several template-free approaches have been proposed that aim to detect candidate particles in an unsupervised manner, without relying on external references~\cite{heimowitz2018apple}. %However, these methods generally perform less effectively under low SNR conditions. 
An alternative strategy within the template-based paradigm is to refine the similarity measures used during matching. Instead of standard cross-correlation, more robust alternatives, such as cross-entropy-based criteria~\cite{sorzano2010clustering}, have been suggested. While promising, the development and validation of such metrics remain largely open challenges.

Taken together, these approaches represent complementary strategies for reducing confirmation bias in particle picking, ranging from algorithmic adjustments to theoretical diagnostics. Continued development of such tools will be essential for ensuring the reliability of cryo-EM reconstructions, especially in low-SNR regimes.


\subsection{Future work}

While the theoretical framework developed here lays the groundwork for understanding confirmation bias in single-particle reconstruction, several important directions remain open for future research.

First, a natural extension of our model involves incorporating in-plane alignment, both rotational and translational, as it is an essential component of modern 2D classification algorithms. This would enable a more comprehensive analysis of bias introduced during iterative alignment procedures. Second, real-world 2D classification often has a mismatch between the number of templ, and and the number of classes, leading to centroid merging and other nonlinear effects not captured by current models. Third, our current analysis assumes white Gaussian noise, whereas actual cryo-EM micrographs are dominated by colored and structured noise stemming from factors such as the microscope's point spread function, and background ice contamination. Finally, an important future work direction is to theoretically analyze the case where true particles are embedded in low-SNR micrographs. Overall, modeling such settings more accurately would enhance the applicability of our framework to real experimental conditions.

\paragraph{Extension to 3D reconstruction in cryo-EM.}
The theoretical framework presented here regarding the cryo-EM process applies to a 2D classification process modeled as a GMM. It is important to note that in cryo-EM, the different projections (or class centers) exhibit a specific algebraic structure. This structure arises from the statistical generative model of the observations:
\begin{align}
    y_i = \Pi \p{R_i \cdot V} + \epsilon, \label{eqn:cryoEMwithProjections}
\end{align}
where $\Pi$ denotes the tomographic projection operator, $R_i$ represents a random 3D rotation operator, such that $R_i \cdot V \triangleq V\p{R_i^{-1} x}$, $V$ is the underlying 3D volume, and 
$\epsilon$ accounts for additive Gaussian noise. In other words, all observations are noisy projections of randomly rotated versions of the same volume $V$.

An important extension of the theoretical framework is to analyze the full 3D reconstruction problem, in which the objective is to recover the volume $V$, from the extracted particles $\{y_i\}$ defined in~\eqref{eqn:cryoEMwithProjections}. This leads to a central question for future research:
To what extent does the estimated volume reflect the actual structure present in the data versus the template volume $V_{\s{template}}$ that was used during particle picking? Addressing this question would provide deeper insight into how early-stage biases introduced by template-based picking propagate through the full cryo-EM pipeline and affect final structural interpretations.


