\section{Setup of the numerical experiments} 
\label{sec:methods}
%\subsection{Template-matching in micrographs with overlapping candidate particles}
In the theoretical model presented in Section~\ref{sec:model}, candidate particles $\{y_i\}_{i=0}^{N-1}$ were assumed to be drawn i.i.d. from an underlying distribution. However, in practical particle picking from micrographs, candidates are not sampled i.i.d.; instead, they are extracted using a sliding-window approach that introduces spatial overlap between neighboring regions and induces statistical dependencies.
Furthermore, when selecting particles, it is desirable to avoid redundancy by ensuring that the selected patches do not overlap. To achieve this, the particle detection procedure must enforce a non-overlapping constraint: only one candidate patch should be selected from any set of overlapping regions. The algorithm used to implement this constraint is described in detail in Algorithm~\ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatchingWithOverlapping}.


This algorithm identifies candidate particles in a collection of 2D micrographs $\{Y_i\}_{i=0}^{R-1} \subset \mathbb{R}^{k \times k}$ by computing their cross-correlation with a set of template images $\{x_\ell\}_{\ell=0}^{L-1} \subset \mathbb{R}^{d \times d}$. For each micrograph-template pair, a 2D circular cross-correlation (assuming periodic boundary conditions) is computed to produce a heatmap $C_{\ell,i}$, where each pixel indicates the similarity between a local patch of the micrograph and the template. Each $C_{\ell,i}$ has the same spatial resolution as the micrograph.
To consolidate the response across templates, the algorithm computes a combined heat map $C_i$ by taking the maximum pixel over all $L$ templates. This results in a score map where each entry reflects the strongest template match at that location. The pixels in $C_i$ are then flattened and sorted in descending order by score.
The algorithm then iterates through the ranked pixels and selects particle candidates that (i) exceed a predefined threshold $T$, and (ii) do not spatially overlap with any previously selected patch. A binary mask is maintained to track used regions and enforce non-overlap. Only the highest-scoring, non-overlapping patches are added to the output set $\mathcal{A}$.
This non-overlapping template matching strategy is commonly used in cryo-EM; see, for example,~\cite{eldar2024object}.


\begin{algorithm}[t!]
\caption{\texttt{Template-Matching Particle Picker with Overlap}}
\label{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatchingWithOverlapping}
\textbf{Input:} Templates $\{x_\ell\}_{\ell=0}^{L-1} \subset \mathbb{R}^{d \times d}$,
         Micrographs $\{Y_i\}_{i=0}^{R-1} \subset \mathbb{R}^{k \times k}$,
         Threshold $T \in \mathbb{R}$ \\
\textbf{Output:} Set $\mathcal{A}$ of extracted particle patches
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State $\mathcal{A} \gets \emptyset$
\For{$i = 0$ to $R - 1$} \Comment{Loop over micrographs}
    \State $C_i \gets \text{zero matrix in } \mathbb{R}^{k \times k}$ \Comment{Heatmap storing the maximum cross-correlation}
    \For{$\ell = 0$ to $L - 1$} \Comment{Loop over templates}
        \State $C_{\ell,i} \gets x_\ell \star Y_i$ \Comment{2D circular cross-correlation}
        \ForAll{pixels $(u,v)$}
            \State $C_i[u,v] \gets \max(C_i[u,v], C_{\ell,i}[u,v])$ \Comment{Maximum of the heatmap}
        \EndFor
    \EndFor
    \State $(S_i, I_i) \gets \textsc{SortDescend}(\textsc{Flatten}(C_i))$ \Comment{Sort the heatmap}
    \State $M_i \gets \text{zero mask in } \{0,1\}^{k \times k}$ \Comment{Binary map tracking already selected regions}
    \For{$m = 0$ to $k^2 - 1$} \Comment{Loop over pixels}
        \State $(u, v) \gets \textsc{IndexToCoords}(I_i[m], k)$ \Comment{Convert flat index to 2D coordinates}
        \If{$S_i[m] > T$ \textbf{and} patch around $(u,v)$ of size $d \times d$ does not overlap in $M_i$} 
            \State $P \gets Y_i[u - d/2 : u + d/2,\, v - d/2 : v + d/2]$
            \State $\mathcal{A} \gets \mathcal{A} \cup \{P\}$
            \State Fill with ones the region of size $d \times d$ around $(u,v)$ in $M_i$
        \EndIf
    \EndFor
\EndFor
\State \Return $\mathcal{A}$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}


\section{Statistical models}
 \label{sec:theory}

\subsection{Preliminaries}
 \label{sec:problemFormulation}

The particle picker's role is to classify each patch as either a particle or noise assuming no overlap between patches. 
Each micrograph patch, or equivalently, candidate particle is represented as $\{y_i\}_{i=0}^{N-1} \in \mathbb{R}^d$, where $N$ is the number of candidate particles, and $d$ is their dimension (which may correspond to flattened 2D or 3D image patches).
Accordingly, the particle-picking task is defined by a binary function $f: \mathbb{R}^d \to \{0,1\}$:  
\begin{align}
    f(y_i) = 
    \begin{cases} 
        1, & \text{if } y_i \text{ is a particle}, \\
        0, & \text{otherwise}.
    \end{cases}
\end{align}


To analyze confirmation bias in particle picking, we model the candidate particles $\{y_i\}_{i=0}^{N-1}$ as pure noise: $\{y_i\}_{i=0}^{N-1} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2 I_{d \times d})$, where $\mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2 I_{d \times d})$ denotes multivariate Gaussian distribution with mean 0, and diagonal covariance matrix with variance $\sigma^2$. We consider a particle picker based on \textit{template matching}, as outlined in Algorithm~\ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}. This picker operates by cross-correlating each observation $\{y_i\}_{i=0}^{N-1}$ with $L$ predefined templates, denoted $\{x_\ell\}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$. Templates are assumed to be normalized so that $\|x_\ell\|_2 = 1$ for all $\ell \in \{0, \ldots, L-1\}$. If the correlation with at least one template exceeds a threshold $T$, the observation is classified as a particle and added to the set of selected particles, $\mathcal{A}$. We denote by $M =\abs{\mathcal{A}}$ the number of selected particles. 

To examine the confirmation bias introduced during the particle selection stage, it is necessary to establish a metric to compare the extracted particles with the initial templates used in the template selection process. Specifically, we analyze two approaches applied to the particles extracted during this stage: one is based on the 2D classification output (relevant for cryo-EM) and the other is based on the 3D reconstruction process (relevant for sub-tomogram averaging in cryo-ET).
In Section \ref{sec:2Dclass}, we focus on the 2D classification output, modeled using Gaussian mixture models (GMM). We analyze the relationship between the maximum-likelihood estimates of the GMM means and the predefined templates employed during particle picking.
In Section \ref{sec:app_3DRecon}, we turn to cryo-ET and investigate the connection between the 3D template volume used in the particle-picking stage and the maximum-likelihood estimator derived from the 3D reconstruction process output. This analysis aims to uncover how the initial templates shape the reconstruction results and whether they contribute to biases in the reconstruction workflow.


\subsection{2D classification of the particles extracted in cryo-EM} \label{sec:2Dclass}

In cryo-EM, following the particle-picking stage, the extracted particles are typically subjected to a 2D classification process. This step groups particles into clusters based on their similarity, with each cluster represented by a centroid, which is the average of the observations assigned to that cluster. These centroids serve two primary purposes: they help assess the overall quality of the dataset and provide a basis for iteratively sorting out false positives or poorly aligned particles. In practice, the appearance of particle-like features in the centroids is often interpreted by researchers as evidence that the selected particles contain meaningful structural information.

\subsubsection{2D Classification based on Gaussian Mixture Models} 
During the 2D classification process, particles are often modeled using a GMM, a widely used and effective probabilistic framework for describing particle heterogeneity. While alternative models exist, we focus on the GMM due to its analytical tractability. In this framework, the observed data are assumed to be generated from a mixture of Gaussian distributions, with each component representing a distinct subpopulation within the dataset. Formally, the realizations of the extracted particles are modeled as:
\begin{align}
    z_0, z_1, \ldots, z_{M-1} \stackrel{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \sum_{\ell=0}^{L-1} w_\ell \cdot \mathcal{N}(\mu_\ell, \Sigma_\ell), \label{eqn:GMMmodel}
\end{align}
where $z_0, z_1, \ldots, z_{M-1}$ are the realizations of the \textit{postulated} GMM distribution of the extracted particles, $w_\ell$, $\mu_\ell$, and $\Sigma_\ell$ are the weight, mean, and covariance matrix of the $\ell$-th Gaussian component, respectively. The weights $\ppp{w_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$ satisfy $w_\ell \geq 0$, and $\sum_{\ell=0}^{L-1} w_\ell = 1$, and are assumed to be known. For the purpose of theoretical modeling, we assume that the number of templates used in the particle-picking process is equal to the number of classes in the GMM, denoted by $L$. In Section \ref{sec:empirical}, we provide empirical results that demonstrate the impact of relaxing this assumption. This assumption is formally stated below as Assumption~\ref{assump:0}.

\begin{assum}\label{assump:0}
The number of templates used in the particle extraction algorithm (Algorithm \ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}) and the number of classes assumed in the GMM during the 2D classification process \eqref{eqn:GMMmodel} are equal to $L$.
\end{assum}

\begin{remark}
    Throughout the Appendix, we use the notation $\{y_i\}_{i=0}^{N-1}$ to denote the candidate particles provided as input to the template-matching particle picker. The resulting set of selected particles, which serves as the input for downstream tasks such as 2D classification and 3D reconstruction, is denoted by $\{z_i\}_{i=0}^{M-1}$.
\end{remark}


\subsubsection{The log-likelihood function of GMM}
In cryo-EM, it is common to assume that all components of the GMM share the same covariance matrix. Furthermore, we assume that this covariance is diagonal and identical across all $L$ components, i.e., $\Sigma_\ell = \sigma_{\s{GMM}}^2 I_{d \times d}$ for all $\ell \in \pp{L}$. The objective of the 2D classification process is to estimate the maximum-likelihood centroids $\ppp{\mu_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$ in \eqref{eqn:GMMmodel}.
In this context, the likelihood of the GMM specified in \eqref{eqn:GMMmodel} is given by:
\begin{align}
    \nonumber \mathcal{L}\p{\ppp{\mu_\ell}_{\ell \in \pp{L}}; \mathcal{A} = \ppp{z_i}_{i \in \pp{M}}} & = \prod_{i=0}^{M-1} f \p{z_i \mid \ppp{\mu_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}}
    \\ & = \prod_{i=0}^{M-1} \sum_{\ell=0}^{L-1} w_\ell \cdot \mathcal{N}\p{z_i \mid \mu_\ell, \Sigma_\ell = \sigma_{\s{GMM}}^2 I_{d \times d}}, \label{eqn:maximumLiklehoodTermMain}
\end{align}
where we have defined, 
\begin{align}
    f\p{z \mid \ppp{\mu_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}} \triangleq \sum_{\ell=0}^{L-1} w_\ell  f_{\ell}(z) = \sum_{\ell=0}^{L-1} w_\ell \cdot \mathcal{N}\p{z \mid \mu_\ell, \Sigma_\ell = \sigma_{\s{GMM}}^2 I_{d \times d}}. \label{eqn:GMMmodelExcplicitMain}
\end{align}
\begin{comment}and the log-likelihood is then given by
\begin{align}
    \ell(\ppp{\mu_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}; \mathcal{A}) = \sum_{i=0}^{M-1} \log \left( \sum_{\ell=0}^{L-1} w_\ell \cdot \mathcal{N}(z_i | \mu_\ell, \Sigma_\ell) \right), \label{eqn:GMMlogLikelihood}
\end{align}
\end{comment}
$\mathcal{N}(z_i | \mu_\ell, \Sigma_\ell)$ represents the multivariate Gaussian density for the $\ell$-th component, and the weights $w_\ell$ are the unknown mixture probabilities for each component. The objective of the 2D classification process is to estimate the centroids $\ppp{\mu_\ell}_{\ell = 0}^{L-1}$, using the maximum likelihood estimator that maximizes the likelihood \eqref{eqn:maximumLiklehoodTermMain}. 
The maximum-likelihood centroids, denoted by $\hat{\mu}_\ell$, are formally defined as
\begin{align}
    \ppp{\hat{\mu}_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1} = \argmax_{\ppp{\mu_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}} \mathcal{L}(\ppp{\mu_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}; \mathcal{A}). \label{eqn:maximumOfLogLikelihood}
\end{align}

Thus, the aim of the 2D classification is to estimate the centroids of the GMM, defined in \eqref{eqn:GMMmodel} using the extracted unlabeled particles $\mathcal{A} = \ppp{z_i}_{i=0}^{M-1}$ 
(i.e., using the output particles of Algorithm~\ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}). The main question we address in this subsection is how the estimated centroids $\ppp{\hat{\mu}_{\ell}}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$ relate to the templates $\ppp{x_{\ell}}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$ used in the particle-picker template-matching (Algorithm \ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}), when the input micrograph is pure noise.

\begin{remark}
We use the notation $\sigma_{\s{GMM}}^2$ to denote the variance of the postulated GMM model described in~\eqref{eqn:GMMmodel}, and $\sigma^2$ to denote the variance of the noise in the input to the particle picker, as described in Algorithm \ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}. These two variances are not necessarily equal in our analysis.
\end{remark}


\subsubsection{The extracted particles form a non-Gaussian mixture model} \label{sec:misspecifiedModels}

Although the extracted particles are often assumed to follow a Gaussian distribution, as defined in \eqref{eqn:GMMmodel}, the statistical properties of the particle picking process described in Algorithm~\ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching} deviate from this assumption. Formally, the probability density function of the particles extracted by Algorithm~\ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching} forms a mixture model that can be expressed as follows:
\begin{align}
    g(z) = \sum_{\ell=0}^{L-1} \pi_\ell \cdot g_\ell(z), \label{eqn:mixtureModelParticlePicker}
\end{align}
where $L$ is the number of components (i.e., the number of templates), $\pi_\ell$ are the mixing coefficients satisfying $\sum_{\ell=0}^{L-1} \pi_\ell = 1$ and $\pi_\ell \geq 0$. Here, $\ppp{g_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}: \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}$ are the probability density functions of the individual template-matching components, with $z \in \mathbb{R}^d$, given by:
\begin{align}
    g_\ell(z) = C_\ell(T) \cdot \exp\p{-\frac{\norm{z}_F^2}{2\sigma^2}} \mathbbm{1}_{\langle z, x_\ell \rangle \geq T}. \label{eqn:componentOfMixtureModelParticlePicker}
\end{align}
where $C_\ell(T) \triangleq 1/\mathbb{P} \pp{\langle z, x_\ell \rangle \geq T}$ is a normalization factor satisfying $\int_{z \in \mathbb{R}^d} dz \ g_\ell(z) = 1$. In other words, $g_\ell$ represents the distribution from which a particle associated with the $\ell$-th template is selected, meaning its correlation with the template $x_\ell$ exceeds the threshold $T$. Clearly, $g_\ell(z)$ does not follow a Gaussian distribution as it is strictly positive only for $z \in \mathbb{R}^d$ values which satisfy $\langle z, x_\ell \rangle \geq T$. As shown in Proposition~\ref{thm:prop1}, the normalization constants are identical across all templates, i.e., $C_{\ell_1}(T) = C_{\ell_2}(T)$ for any $\ell_1,\ell_2 \in \pp{L}$, so we simplify the notation by writing $C_T = C_\ell(T)$. 
Figure~\ref{fig:appendix_B1} illustrates the probability density function of this truncated Gaussian distribution in one and two dimensions.

\begin{figure}[t!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{appendix_truncatedGaussian.pdf}
    \caption{\textbf{Template-matching-induced truncation of a Gaussian distribution.} 
    \textbf{(a)} In one dimension, the original Gaussian density (dashed) is truncated by a hard threshold $T$, resulting in a re-normalized density $g_\ell(z)$ supported only on the region where $z \geq T$. 
    \textbf{(b)} In two dimensions, the original isotropic Gaussian is truncated to the half-space defined by $\langle z, x_\ell \rangle \geq T$, where $x_\ell$ is the template direction (shown as a red arrow) and the dashed line denotes the threshold boundary.}
    \label{fig:appendix_B1}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Misspecification of true statistics and the GMM model.} It is important to recognize that the statistical assumptions underlying the particles, specifically, the GMM statistics defined in \eqref{eqn:GMMmodel}, do not accurately reflect the true statistics of the particle picking process based on template matching, as described in \eqref{eqn:mixtureModelParticlePicker} and \eqref{eqn:componentOfMixtureModelParticlePicker}. Consequently, \textit{the models are misspecified}, and this must be considered when estimating the centroids.


\subsubsection{Relationship between 2D classification centers and templates}  
The model presented here raises two central questions: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item What is the relationship between the mean of each component $g_\ell$ in the true mixture model \eqref{eqn:componentOfMixtureModelParticlePicker} and the corresponding template $x_\ell$ when the number of particles $M \to \infty$? (answered in Proposition \ref{thm:prop1})
    \item In the 2D classification process based on a GMM model, how do the templates $\{x_\ell\}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$ relate to the GMM maximum likelihood estimators of the means $\{\hat{\mu}_\ell\}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$, as specified in \eqref{eqn:maximumOfLogLikelihood}? (answered in Theorem \ref{thm:classesCentersVersusTemplates})
\end{enumerate}



Proposition \ref{thm:prop1} examines the relationship between the mean of a single component $g_\ell$ in the mixture model \eqref{eqn:componentOfMixtureModelParticlePicker} and the corresponding template $x_\ell$. In other words, it relates between the mean of all extracted particles whose correlation with the $\ell$-th template $x_\ell$ exceeds the threshold $T$, and the template $x_\ell$ itself.
We show that, in this case, as the number of particles increases ($M \to \infty$), the mean of these particles converges to the template $x_\ell$, scaled by a factor $\alpha \geq T$, which is bounded below by the threshold used in the template-matching process. Notably, this convergence holds without imposing any specific constraints on the threshold $T$ in Algorithm \ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}. Furthermore, in the second part of the proposition, we show that for asymptotically large thresholds $T$, the ratio $\alpha / T \to 1$, indicating that $\alpha$ approaches the lower bound.


The second result, Theorem \ref{thm:classesCentersVersusTemplates}, demonstrates that the output of the 2D classification process reproduces the templates used during the particle-picking stage under two conditions: a large number of particles ($M \to \infty$) and a sufficiently large template-matching threshold~$T$. The necessity of a high threshold is illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:3}, and discussed in the main text.


\subsection{3D reconstruction of the particles extracted in cryo-ET} \label{sec:app_3DRecon}

\paragraph{Subtomogram averaging in cryo-ET.} 
The cryo-ET model assumes a 3D tomogram composed of 3D subtomograms located at unknown locations, where each subtomogram is a noisy, randomly rotated version of the same underlying volume $V$. Specifically, each subtomogram $z_i$, 
is modeled as:
\begin{align}
    z_i = R_i \cdot V + \epsilon_i, \label{eqn:3DcryoETmodel}
\end{align}  
where $V$ is the underlying 3D volume, $\{R_i\}_{i} \in \s{SO}(3)$ are random 3D rotations, and $\ppp{\epsilon_i}_{i=0}^{M-1} \overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_{V}^2 I_{d \times d})$ represents additive centered Gaussian noise with variance $\sigma_V^2$. 
In the cryo-ET computational pipeline, subtomograms are first detected and extracted from the tomogram during the particle-picking stage, followed by a 3D reconstruction process used to estimate the volume $V$ from the extracted subtomograms $\ppp{z_i}_{i=0}^{M-1}$. Below, we describe the typical particle-picking and 3D reconstruction processes, along with the assumptions regarding their relationship.

\begin{remark}
We use the notation $\sigma_V^2$ to denote the variance of the postulated model described in~\eqref{eqn:3DcryoETmodel}, and $\sigma^2$ to denote the variance of the noise in the input to the particle picker, as described in Algorithm \ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}. These two variances are not necessarily equal in our analysis.
\end{remark}

\paragraph{Templates generation in cryo-ET.}
During the particle-picking stage in cryo-ET, $L$ samples from the group of 3D rotations $\s{SO}(3)$
are used to generate the templates from an underlying 3D volume template $V_{\s{template}}$, i.e., $x_\ell = R_\ell \cdot V_{\s{template}}$, for $\ell \in \pp{L}$. These templates are then used as inputs to Algorithm \ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}. The statistics of the output set $\mathcal{A}$ of the template-matching particle picker algorithm (Algorithm \ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}), when applied to pure Gaussian noise tomograms, follow the distribution
\begin{align}
  g_{\s{picker}}\p{z} \triangleq \sum_{\ell=0}^{L-1} \pi (R_\ell) \cdot g_\ell(z) = C_T  \sum_{\ell=0}^{L-1} \ \pi(R_\ell) \cdot \exp\p{-\frac{\norm{z}^2}{2 \sigma^2} } \mathbbm{1}_{\langle z, R_\ell \cdot V_{\s{template}} \rangle \geq T},  \label{eqn:actualETstatistics}
\end{align}
where $\pi \p{R_\ell}$ is the actual prior distribution of the samples $\ppp{z_i}_{i=0}^{M-1}$ for the $L$ different rotations $\ppp{R_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$, $g_\ell(z)$ is defined in \eqref{eqn:componentOfMixtureModelParticlePicker}, and $C_T = 1/{\mathbb{P} \pp{\langle z, R_\ell \cdot V_{\s{template}} \rangle \geq T}}$ is a normalization factor. 

\paragraph{The 3D reconstruction process.} 
The aim of the 3D reconstruction process is to estimate the volume $V$ from the observations (subtomograms) $\ppp{z_i}_{i \in \pp{M}}$ specified in \eqref{eqn:3DcryoETmodel}. Below, we derive the explicit likelihood function.

Let $\mathcal{A} = \ppp{z_i}_{i \in \pp{M}}$ be subtomograms follows the model in \eqref{eqn:3DcryoETmodel}. 
Let $V$ denote the underlying  volume given in the process defined in \eqref{eqn:3DcryoETmodel}. Let $f_\s{ET}\p{z; V}$ represent the probability distribution function of the model in \eqref{eqn:3DcryoETmodel}:
\begin{align}
    f_{\s{ET}} \p{z; V} = \frac{1}{\p{2\pi\sigma_V^2}^{d/2}} \int_{R \in \s{SO}(3)} \ p(R) \cdot \exp\p{-\frac{\norm{z - \p{R \cdot V}}^2}{2 \sigma_V^2} } dR, \label{eqn:distributionCryoETfor3Dalign}
\end{align}
where $p(R)$ represents the prior probability distribution of the rotations $R \in \s{SO}(3)$.
Then, the likelihood function of the model \eqref{eqn:3DcryoETmodel} is given by: 
\begin{align}
  \nonumber \mathcal{L}_\s{postulated} & \p{V ; \mathcal{A} = \ppp{z_i}_{i \in \pp{M}}} = \prod_{i=0}^{M-1} f_{\s{ET}} \p{z_i; V}
  \\ & =  \frac{1}{\p{2\pi\sigma_V^2}^{dM/2}} \prod_{i=0}^{M-1} \int_{R \in \s{SO}(3)} \ p(R) \cdot \exp\p{-\frac{\norm{z_i - \p{R \cdot V}}^2}{2 \sigma_V^2} } dR. \label{eqn:assumedLikeEst3DcryoETIntegral}
\end{align}


The 3D reconstruction process typically involves iterative methods (such as expectation-maximization) to estimate the volume $V$ from the 3D extracted particles \eqref{eqn:3DcryoETmodel}. During these iterative methods, a discretization of $\s{SO}(3)$ is performed, which we denote by $\ppp{R_n}_{n} \in \s{SO}(3)$. Then, we approximate the likelihood function of \eqref{eqn:assumedLikeEst3DcryoETIntegral},
\begin{align}
  \nonumber \bar{\mathcal{L}}_\s{postulated} & \p{V ; \mathcal{A} = \ppp{z_i}_{i=0}^{M-1}} 
  \\ & =  \frac{1}{\p{2\pi\sigma_V^2}^{dM/2}} \prod_{i=0}^{M-1} \sum_{R_n \in \s{SO}(3)} \ w(R_n) \cdot \exp\p{-\frac{\norm{z_i - \p{R_n \cdot V}}^2}{2 \sigma_V^2} }.  \label{eqn:assumedLikeEst3DcryoET}
\end{align}
In addition, we represent the corresponding probability density function associated with this discretization, following from \eqref{eqn:distributionCryoETfor3Dalign} by, 
\begin{align}
    \bar{f}_{\s{ET}} \p{z; V} = \frac{1}{\p{2\pi\sigma_V^2}^{d/2}} \sum_{R_n \in \s{SO}(3)} \ w(R_n) \cdot \exp\p{-\frac{\norm{z - \p{R_n \cdot V}}^2}{2 \sigma_V^2} }. \label{eqn:distributionCryoETfor3DalignDisc}
\end{align}
The maximum likelihood estimator in this case is given by:
\begin{align}
    \hat{V} =  \argmax_{V} \bar{\mathcal{L}}_{\s{postulated}}(V; \mathcal{A}). \label{eqn:maximumOfLogLikelihoodCryoET}
\end{align}
It is important to note that the \textit{postulated} statistics of the subtomograms, as specified in \eqref{eqn:distributionCryoETfor3Dalign}, differ from the \textit{actual} statistics of the output from the particle-picking stage, as given in \eqref{eqn:actualETstatistics}. Therefore, the model is misspecified, similar to the GMM case discussed in the previous section.

The following assumption states that the same set of $\mathrm{SO}(3)$ samples is used both in the 3D reconstruction process and to generate the templates in the particle-picking stage. Specifically, the rotations $\{R_\ell\}_{\ell \in \{0, \ldots, L-1\}}$ used during particle picking, i.e., $x_\ell = R_\ell \cdot V_{\s{template}}$, are identical to the discretization $\{R_n\}_n$ of $\mathrm{SO}(3)$ employed in the reconstruction procedure, as defined in \eqref{eqn:distributionCryoETfor3DalignDisc}.

\begin{assum}\label{assump:1}
The $\mathrm{SO}(3)$ samples used for generating the templates in the particle-picking stage are the same as the samples employed during the 3D reconstruction process.
\end{assum}

The primary question we address concerns the relationship between $V_{\mathrm{template}}$, the 3D template volume used during the particle-picking stage, and $\hat{V}$, the output of the 3D reconstruction process, represented as the maximum likelihood estimator $\hat{V}$ defined in \eqref{eqn:maximumOfLogLikelihoodCryoET}. At first glance, one might expect the reconstructed volume to converge to zero since the underlying candidate particles are zero-mean Gaussian noise. However, Theorem~\ref{thm:3DvolumeVsTemplateVolume} reveals a surprising result: as the number of extracted particles $M \to \infty$ and for asymptotic threshold $T \to \infty$, the reconstructed 3D volume $\hat{V}$ converges to the template 3D volume $V_{\mathrm{template}}$, up to a scaling factor.

\section{Theoretical results} \label{sec:theoreticalResults}
\subsection{Particle picking based on a single template matching}
We begin by presenting a theoretical result that establishes the relationship between the mean of the $\ell$-th component of the mixture model, as defined in \eqref{eqn:componentOfMixtureModelParticlePicker}, and the corresponding template $x_\ell$.

\paragraph{The centers of the template matching process. } Using the definitions in Algorithm~\ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}, let $m_\ell^{(T)}$ be defined as:
\begin{align}
m_\ell^{(T)} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{A}_\ell^{(T)}|} \sum_{z_i \in \mathcal{A}_\ell^{(T)}} z_i, \label{eq:mu_l_def}
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}
\mathcal{A}_\ell^{(T)} = \{z_i \in \mathcal{A} \mid \langle z_i, x_\ell \rangle \geq T\}. \label{eq:calA_ell}
\end{align}
Here, $m_\ell^{(T)}$ represents the centroid of the particles whose correlation with the template $x_\ell$ exceeds the threshold $T$. The key question we address is: what is the relationship between $m_\ell^{(T)}$ and the corresponding template $x_\ell$?

To this end, it would be beneficial to define, for every $\ell \in \pp{L}$, the following sets:
    \begin{align}
        \mathcal{V}_\ell^{(T)} \triangleq \ppp{
        v \in \mathbb{R}^d: {{\langle{v, x_\ell\rangle}} \geq T } }\label{eqn:VlDef}.
    \end{align}
Note that $\ppp{\mathcal{V}_\ell^{(T)}}_{\ell\in[L]}$ are deterministic, and are not necessarily disjoint sets.

\begin{proposition}
\label{thm:prop1}
Let $\ppp{y_i}_{i=0}^{N-1} \stackrel{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2 I_{d \times d})$ represent the input candidate particles to Algorithm \ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}. Let $\ppp{x_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$ denote the normalized templates, $T$ the threshold, and $\mathcal{A}$ the output set of selected particles in Algorithm \ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}.
Define $m_\ell^{(T)}$ as given in \eqref{eq:mu_l_def}, and $\mathcal{A}_\ell^{(T)}$ as the corresponding set, specified in \eqref{eq:calA_ell}. Then,
\begin{enumerate}
    \item For $N \to \infty$,
    \begin{align}
        m_\ell^{(T)} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{A}_\ell^{(T)}|} \sum_{y_i \in \mathcal{A}_\ell^{(T)}} y_i \xrightarrow[]{\s{a.s.}} \alpha \cdot x_\ell, \label{eqn:mu_ell_asymptotic_propoerty}
    \end{align}
    for a constant $\alpha \geq T$, independent of $\ell$. 
    \item For $N \to \infty$, and $T \to \infty$, 
    \begin{align}
        \lim_{T \to \infty}\lim_{N \to \infty} \frac{m_\ell^{(T)}}{T} = x_\ell,
    \end{align}
    where the convergence is almost surely, and for every $\ell \in \pp{L}$.
\end{enumerate}


\end{proposition}

This result proves that averaging the noise observations $\ppp{y_i}_{i=0}^{N-1}$, whose correlation with the template $x_\ell$ exceeds a specified threshold, converges almost surely to the template $x_\ell$, scaled by a factor $\alpha$. The scaling factor $\alpha$ is guaranteed to be at least as large as the threshold used in the template-matching process. Furthermore, the second part of the result shows that for asymptotically large thresholds $T$, the ratio $\alpha / T$ approaches 1, meaning $\alpha$ attains the lower bound asymptotically in $T$.
In essence, Proposition \ref{thm:prop1} establishes that the centers of the GMM defined in \eqref{eqn:GMMmodel} resemble the templates, up to a scaling factor. This differs from the "Einstein from noise" phenomenon and the associated confirmation bias in 2D classifications, where the resulting estimates are positively correlated with the initial templates but do not fully replicate them~\cite{balanov2024einstein}.

\subsection{Particle picking based on multiple templates}

In the previous sections, we demonstrated that the particle-picking process generates a mixture model with $L$ components, where $L$ corresponds to the number of templates. The centers of these mixtures reproduce the templates, up to a scaling factor. However, this result does not guarantee that the output of the 2D classification process (based on the maximum likelihood estimation of the GMM defined in \eqref{eqn:maximumOfLogLikelihood}) will directly yield these centroids. The primary goal of this section is to analyze the relationship between the templates $\ppp{x_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$ and the 2D classification outputs $\ppp{\hat{\mu}_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$, as defined in \eqref{eqn:maximumOfLogLikelihood}.

In general, the sequence of maximum likelihood estimators converges in probability to the true value being estimated, provided that the assumed model distribution aligns with the underlying distribution from which the samples are generated \cite{newey1994large}. Therefore, if the underlying distribution of the extracted particles were GMM, the maximum-likelihood centroid estimators $\ppp{\hat{\mu}_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$, as defined in \eqref{eqn:maximumOfLogLikelihood}, would converge to the centroids $\ppp{m_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$, as defined in \eqref{eq:mu_l_def}, assuming the 2D classification process follows a GMM.

However, as discussed in Section \ref{sec:theory}, in our case, while the assumed model is a GMM, the actual statistics of the particle-picking output do not follow Gaussian statistics. Specifically, the distribution of the particle-picking output is described by \eqref{eqn:mixtureModelParticlePicker} and \eqref{eqn:componentOfMixtureModelParticlePicker}. Therefore, additional conditions are required for the maximum-likelihood centroid estimators of the assumed GMM model $\ppp{\hat{\mu}_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$, to converge to the centroids of the true mixture model, $\ppp{m_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$. These conditions are outlined in Theorem \ref{thm:classesCentersVersusTemplates}.


\begin{thm}
[Convergence of 2D class averages to particle-picking templates]
\label{thm:classesCentersVersusTemplates}
Fix $L\geq 2$, and denote the input templates $\ppp{x_\ell}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}$, input threshold $T$, and input observations $\ppp{y_i}_{i=0}^{N-1} \stackrel{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2 I_{d \times d})$ to Algorithm \ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}. Let $\mathcal{A}$ be the output of Algorithm \ref{alg:particlePickerTemplateMatching}, such that $\abs{\mathcal{A}} = M$. Assume that $x_{\ell_1} \neq x_{\ell_2}$ for every $\ell_1 \neq \ell_2$. Let $\ppp{\hat{\mu}_\ell}_{l=0}^{L-1}$ be the maximum-likelihood estimators of the 2D classification process, applied on the extracted particles set $\mathcal{A}$, as defined in \eqref{eqn:maximumOfLogLikelihood}. Then, under Assumption \ref{assump:0}, there exists a permutation function $\pi: \pp{L} \to \pp{L}$, such that for every $0 \leq \ell \leq L-1$,
    \begin{align}
        \lim_{T\to\infty}\lim_{N\to\infty} \frac{\hat{\mu}_{\pi \p{\ell}}} {T} = x_\ell \label{eqn:nonVanishingEstimator},
    \end{align}
where the convergence is in probability.

\end{thm}

Theorem \ref{thm:classesCentersVersusTemplates} demonstrates that, for an asymptotic number of extracted particles $M$ and an asymptotic template-matching threshold $T$, the output of the 2D classification process reproduces the templates used during the particle-picking stage. This reproduction occurs up to a permutation of the output centers, meaning the output centroids are aligned with the templates, but their sequencing order may differ. The requirement for a sufficiently large threshold is crucial, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:3}.

The relation between Proposition~\ref{thm:prop1} and Theorem~\ref{thm:classesCentersVersusTemplates} is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:appendix_C1}, which illustrates the difference between the labeled setting (Proposition~\ref{thm:prop1}), where particle-template associations are known and mean vectors converge directly to scaled templates, and the unlabeled setting (Theorem~\ref{thm:classesCentersVersusTemplates}), where convergence to templates occurs for a high threshold.

