First, we applied the Kruskal-Wallis (KW) test~\cite{kruskal1952use} to the fitness values of the experiment group. Table~\ref{tab:KW_F} presents the p-values obtained from the KW tests on corresponding tasks and LLMs. The KW results indicate that in all the cases of executing SA and TS tasks on the two LLMs, there exist statistically significant differences in distributions of fitness values across the experiment group.

Then, we conducted a pair-wise comparison of the proposed algorithms with the random search using the Mann-Whitney U (MWU) test~\cite{mcknight2010mann}. Due to the multiple comparisons, we adjusted $alpha$ to validate the p-value as 0.005 by using the Bonferroni adjustment~\cite{arcuri2011practical}. The details of the $\alpha$ adjustment are explained in Section~\ref{sec.expr.tv}. The test results of comparing the distribution of fitness values are explained in Table~\ref{tab:MWU}.

Each value in the Table~\ref{tab:MWU} content represents a p-value, with ``T" indicating that the p-value is less than 0.005. These p-values are derived from the MWU test, which compares the distribution of fitness values for outcome sets generated by different GA implementations and those produced by a random search algorithm. The results demonstrate statistically significant differences between the distributions of the search algorithms and the random methods in all cases.

