\begin{abstract}
Negative longitudinal magnetoresistance (NLMR) is a decrease in the electrical resistivity of a material when the driving electric field and an external magnetic field are collinear. In the semiclassical weak field regime, the NLMR of non-magnetic metals is activated in the presence of substantial concentrations of Berry curvature (BC). 
This restricts the appearance of a semi-classical NLMR 
to metals with acentric
%either to materials with  magnetic order or to non-magnetic metals with acentric 
crystalline arrangements, including Weyl semimetals. 
Here, we show a previously unidentified mechanism of semi-classical NLMR that can be present in strongly spin-orbit coupled non-magnetic metals even if the crystal possess bulk inversion symmetry. A Zeeman-activated BC directly couples to the orbital motion of Bloch electrons to generate a negative MR that scales with the relaxation time precisely as the Drude resistivity. Importantly, the Zeeman-activated BC is \emph{independent} of the external magnetic field strength. It is instead related to the degree of non-parabolicity of the spin-orbit coupled electronic bands. We show that this NLMR mechanism, which is independent of the Land\'e $g$-factor, is generic and
appears in \emph{all} centrosymmetric point groups and can occur both in topological and conventional conductors. 
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\paragraph{Introduction --}
Magnetoresistance (MR) refers to a change in the electrical resistivity of material when subject to an externally applied magnetic field ${\bm B}$. In its simplest form, MR in non-magnetic materials
arises as a consequence of the classical Lorentz force exerted on the charge carriers~\cite{abrikosov2017fundamentals}. This orbital MR grows quadratically with  ${\bm B}$ in the weak-field regime and is positive: the electrical resistivity at any finite values of the magnetic field is larger than the zero-field resistivity. Additionally the orbital MR is expected to be strongest when the driving electric field ${\bm E}$ is orthogonal to $\bm B$, and, conversely, to be vanishing when 
${\bm E} \parallel {\bm B}$. 
However, the absence of this ${\bm E} \parallel {\bm B}$  longitudinal magnetoresistance  [c.f. Fig.~\ref{fig:NLMRsketch}] is a result strictly valid for ideal free electrons with isotropic energy dispersion. 
Charge carriers in conventional metals can possess strongly anisotropic dispersions, manifested for instance in multiply connected Fermi surfaces~\cite{Pippard1964}. This can consequently lead to a net longitudinal MR, which, precisely as its transversal counterpart, grows quadratically with the magnetic field strength and satisfies the so-called Kohler relation~\cite{Kohler1938}:  the ratio between the finite field resistivity $\rho({\bm B})$ and the Drude resistivity $\rho_0$ is a function of $\omega_c \tau $ with $\omega_c$ indicating the cyclotron frequency and $\tau$ the relaxation time~\cite{PalHK2010}. 
Therefore, the orbital MR is inherently an extrinsic phenomenon.  
Anisotropic scattering mechanisms \cite{ArgyresPN1956,SondheimerEH1962,JonesMC1967,StroudD1976} and inhomogeneous boundary effects \cite{MarkLee1994,MillerDL1996} are additional sources of positive longitudinal MR. 

