The mechanisms leading to the extrinsic positive longitudinal MR are well understood. The situation is  different for the negative  
%A different 
longitudinal magnetoresistance (NLMR) experimentally observed in a vast class of non-magnetic materials with both non-centrosymmetric~\cite{Huang2015,Arnold2016,YanBinghai2017,Armitage2018} and centrosymmetric~\cite{Wiedmann2016} crystalline arrangements. This NLMR typically violates the Kohler relation, with the ratio $[\rho(B) - \rho(0)]/\rho(0)$ that is independent of disorder. 
%Put differently, the  finite-field resistivity $\rho({\bf B})$ scales as $\tau^{-1}$, just as the Drude resistivity $\rho(0)$. 
For strong magnetic fields, specifically in the quantum regime $\omega_c \tau \gg 1$ with well-formed Landau levels, the intrinsic NLMR of topological Dirac and Weyl semimetals ~\cite{SonDT2013,SpivakB2016,Armitage2018} derives from the chiral anomaly~\cite{Nielsen1983} caused by the $n=0$ Landau level. NLMR at strong magnetic fields has been also demonstrated in conventional centrosymmetric and time-reversal invariant conductors~\cite{Andreev2018,Goswami2015} but only for specific range of parameters. In materials 
 with very large Land\'e $g$-factors, instead, the intrinsic NLMR has been shown to arise from the orbital magnetic moment of the Zeeman-split electronic bands~\cite{DaiXin2017}. 

However, the theoretical understanding of the intrinsic weak-field  NLMR is limited to metals with non-trivial quantum geometric properties~\cite{SonDT2013,SpivakB2016,Sekine2017,NandyS2017,SharmaGargee2017,YangGao2017,JohanssonAnnika2019}, which requires 
a non-centrosymmetric
%either the presence of long-range magnetic order or of a non-centrosymmetric 
crystalline arrangement. In this case, the intrinsic NLMR can be directly linked to the anomalous velocity~\cite{Sundaram1999,GaoYang2014,GaoYang2015} encoded in the Berry curvature $\bm \Omega$ of Bloch electrons. The physical origin of the intrinsic NLMR at weak fields in non-magnetic metals with inversion symmetry is currently missing.

The aim of this Letter is to show a generic
 mechanism of weak-field NLMR, present in \emph{all} centrosymmetric point groups \emph{independent} of the topology of the electronic states. The mechanism we unveil here is only related to the non-parabolicity of spin-orbit coupled electronic bands. The spin-orbit coupling strength governs the emergence of a Zeeman-activated Berry curvature (BC) that is independent of the magnetic field strength and Land\'e $g$-factor, and directly couples to the orbital motion of Bloch electrons to produce a net NLMR. 





\paragraph{Zeeman-activated Berry curvature --} We first discuss the appearance of a Zeeman-activated BC that is independent of the magnetic field strength. To make things concrete, let us consider a crystalline system with the maximally symmetric point group equipped with inversion symmetry: the cubic ${\mathcal O}_h$ group. Additionally, we will consider a single valley system, with the low energy bands centered around the $\Gamma$ point of the Brillouin zone. Due to the concomitant presence of time-reversal symmetry $\Theta$ and inversion symmetry ${\mathcal I}$, all the electronic states are guaranteed to be twofold degenerate, with each pair of bands that are Kramers' related. The effective long wavelength Hamiltonian for a single Kramers' pair can be written as ${\mathcal H}({\bf k})= d_0{(\bf k}) \sigma_0 + \vec{{\bf d}}({\bf k}) \cdot \vec{\boldsymbol{\sigma}}$, with the Pauli matrices $\vec{\boldsymbol \sigma}=(\sigma_x,\sigma_y,\sigma_z)$ acting in spin space and the crystalline momentum polynomials $\vec{{\bf d}}({\bf k})$ constrained by symmetries. Inversion symmetry, for instance, requires that only terms quadratic in momentum ${\bf k}$ are allowed. Together with the fact that the Pauli matrices transform in the $T_{1u}$ three-dimensional irreducible representation of the ${\mathcal O}_h$ group, we then obtain the effective Hamiltonian ${\mathcal H}_0({\bf k})= \hbar^2  \left(k_x^2+k_y^2+k_z^2 \right) \sigma_0 / (2 m^{\star})$ which implies that the two degenerate bands are perfectly parabolic and isotropic with effective mass $m^{\star}$. 

