\begin{abstract}
In many financial prediction problems, the behavior of individual units (such as loans, bonds, or stocks) is influenced by observable unit-level factors and macroeconomic variables, as well as by latent cross-sectional effects. Traditional approaches attempt to capture these latent effects via handcrafted summary features.
We propose a \emph{Set-Sequence model} that eliminates the need for handcrafted features. The Set model first learns a shared cross-sectional summary at each period. The Sequence model then ingests the summary-augmented time series for each unit independently to predict its outcome. Both components are learned jointly over arbitrary sets sampled during training. Our approach harnesses the set nature of the cross-section and is computationally efficient, generating set summaries in linear time relative to the number of units. It is also flexible, allowing the use of existing sequence models and accommodating a variable number of units at inference. Empirical evaluations demonstrate that our Set-Sequence model significantly outperforms benchmarks on stock return prediction and mortgage behavior tasks. Code will be released.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
Important time series problems in finance and economics, such as estimating loan risk and forecasting stock returns, often involve predicting outcomes for a varying number of correlated units. For example, consider predicting the outcomes for a pool of 10,000 loans with 50 features per loan. A full joint model requires handling a total of 500,000 features to capture latent cross-sectional effects such as contagion~\cite{10.1257/aer.104.8.2527, AZIZPOUR2018154, 10.1257/pol.5.2.313}. This is practically intractable, so a common approach in the literature augments unit-level and macroeconomic feature series with manually crafted summary features intended to capture the latent cross-sectional effects (e.g.~\cite{dlmr} employs lagged default rate for each zip code). Aside from requiring domain expertise, this approach does not scale well and does not reliably capture all relevant cross-sectional dynamics. 

This paper proposes a \setseq model for time-series, which \textit{learns} the latent cross-sectional effects, eliminating the need to handcraft features.  Figure~\ref{fig:banner} shows the architecture. Harnessing the exchangeability of units\footnote{Exchangeability means the identity of individual units (e.g. stock ticker)  is irrelevant -- unit-level behavior is determined solely by observed features. Formally, for a problem of size $M$, the unit-level feature vectors $X^i$ are exchangeable:
$P(X^1, X^2, \dots, X^M) = P(X^{\pi(1)}, X^{\pi(2)}, \dots, X^{\pi(M)})$, for any permutation $\pi$. }, the \setseq model first computes an order-invariant set summary at each period, creating a time series of cross-sectional features. This series augments the time series for each unit separately, enabling the model to capture group dynamics without handcrafted features. 
Next, the prediction for each unit is made independently by a sequence model operating on this augmented time series containing both unit-level and cross-sectional features. This permits the use of any preferred sequence model (e.g. Transformer, RNN). Viewed through this lens, the \setseq model makes simple architectural changes that have significant practical implications: it provides a way to extend a unit-specific model to include cross-sectional dependencies without needing to handcraft features, and the model allows inference over a variable number of units. Moreover, the compute scales linearly in the number of units. Section \ref{gen_inst} provides details on our methodology.

\begin{figure*}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{figures/set_seq_model_arch_may_12_neurips.pdf}
    \caption{\label{fig:banner}
     The \setseq model: The Set model estimates a cross-sectional summary at each time, in linear complexity over the number of units. This augments the original features of each unit and the Sequence model consumes this augmented series to make predictions for each unit independently. Note that the Set model has a look-back of $L$ time periods, where $L\geq 1$ is a model parameter. The number of units $M$ and time periods $T$ may vary at inference.}
\end{figure*}

We first benchmark the performance and computational efficiency of the Set-Sequence model using a synthetic task. It is designed to mimic a loan default prediction task with a large number of exchangeable loans (units) whose defaults are driven by a shared low-dimensional latent factor, $\lambda_t$, representing the contagion effects observed in the mortgage, corporate, or consumer setting.
First, we show that the \setseq model significantly outperforms the best state-of-the-art sequence models (e.g. KL divergence between the true and predicted transition probabilities). Second, we show that the model performs near optimally across a wide range of pool sizes, with performance increasing monotonically as coverage increases. Third, we show that the model is interpretable: the Set model learns cross-sectional effects that closely track the true effect $\lambda_t$ ($\rho=0.95$). 
Finally, to motivate the choice of linear aggregation in the Set component, we replace it with a multi-head attention (MHA) layer, an alternative whose cross-sectional cost grows quadratically. On 1,000 units, this MHA variant trains $3.3~\times$ slower and uses $3.5~\times$ more GPU memory than our linear model.

We next evaluate the Set-Sequence model on a stock portfolio prediction task using actual return data covering 36,600 US stocks. The model selects daily portfolio weights to maximize the portfolio’s annual Sharpe ratio (a widely used performance criterion). We compare against state-of-the-art sequence models and the leading task-specific CNN-Transformer of \cite{guijarroordonez2022deeplearningstatisticalarbitrage}. Over a 15-year out-of-sample period, the Set-Sequence model achieves between 22\% and 42\% higher Sharpe ratios compared to baseline models. These improvements are highly significant from an economic standpoint, and translate into substantial investment gains. 

Finally, we evaluate the \setseq model on a large-scale US mortgage risk prediction task, comparing it to a suite of sequence models and the leading task-specific deep learning model of \cite{dlmr}. The task involves classifying the monthly mortgage state (current, 30/60/90 delinquency, foreclosure, prepayment) given its history.  The data cover 5 million loan-month observations, and contain 52 dynamic loan-level and macro-economic features.
Efficiently harnessing cross-sectional patterns, the \setseq model achieves significantly higher AUCs than the benchmark models. The model delivers more accurate risk metrics, enabling more effective risk management.



Our work underscores the importance of exploiting exchangeability in high-dimensional time series and shows that the \setseq model is an effective tool for tackling this. Our model: (1) {\it Outperforms strong benchmarks} on both synthetic and real tasks. It accommodates a variable number of units at inference, which is of significant practical interest. (2) \textit{Efficiently} addresses large problems due to its linear scaling along the cross-sectional dimension. (3) Is \textit{interpretable} with a low-dimensional set summary offering a window into the dependency structure across units.


\section{Related Literature}
Traditional approaches to modeling multiple time series have relied on, for example, Vector Autoregressive (VAR) models \cite{88b5a24f-f69b-397a-bc87-27f2b49c5503}, which scale quadratically in the cross-sectional dimension. Linear factor models \cite{7b0a91c7-827c-3a98-b60f-2fdc38fae890, doi:10.1198/073500102317351921, stock2011dynamic, stock2002forecasting, bok2018macroeconomic} offer an improvement, greatly reducing dimensionality compared to classical VAR approaches. While these linear models struggle with non-linear dependencies, deep learning approaches to factor modeling \cite{pmlr-v97-wang19k} have emerged to address non-linearities, though they still require retraining when new units are introduced.

Recent deep learning architectures, while powerful, fail to exploit cross-sectional exchangeability while maintaining computational efficiency. Transformer variants \cite{vaswani2017attention, zhou2021informerefficienttransformerlong,liu2022pyraformer,zhang2023crossformer, liu2024itransformerinvertedtransformerseffective, zhou2022fedformerfrequencyenhanceddecomposed,wu2022autoformerdecompositiontransformersautocorrelation, 10.1145/3637528.3671928}, State Space Models 
such as S4~\cite{gu2022efficiently}, H3~\cite{fu2022hungry}, and others~\cite{zhang2023effectivelymodelingtimeseries, WANG2025129178},  convolution based models (LongConv~\cite{fu2023simple}, Hyena~\cite{poli2023hyena}, and Multilayer Perceptrons \cite{chen2023tsmixerallmlparchitecturetime, NEURIPS2023_f1d16af7} all scale quadratically in the cross-sectional dimension. This often necessitates modeling each unit individually with shared parameters, relying on handcrafted features to capture joint dependencies as in \cite{dlmr}.



Some approaches have attempted to capture cross-sectional exchangeability more directly. \cite{salinas2019highdimensionalmultivariateforecastinglowrank} combine a local recurrent neural network (RNN) model with a global Gaussian Copula model, but their simple covariance structure may not capture non-linear joint dependencies and has only been demonstrated for single-feature units. Other work explores capturing global dependencies through low-rank matrix factorization trained iteratively alongside forecasting models \cite{NEURIPS2019_3a0844ce}, but this also needs refitting when new units are introduced. There is also research that exploits set structures, introduced by \cite{DBLP:journals/corr/ZaheerKRPSS17, lee2019set}, though it focuses on set representations across time rather than in the cross-section~\cite{horn2020setfunctionstimeseries}. We provide a more in-depth literature review in Appendix~\ref{app:lit_review}.


\section{Set-Sequence Model}
\label{gen_inst}

Consider a dataset  
$\mathcal{D} = \{({Y}^{i}, {X}^{i}) \}_{i=1}^M$ with $M$ units, where $Y^{i} = (Y_{1}^{i}, Y_{2}^{i}, \dots, Y_{T}^{i}) \in \mathbb{R}^{d_y \times T}$ is the response and $X^{i} = (X_{1}^{i}, X_{2}^{i}, \dots, X_{T}^{i}) \in \mathbb{R}^{d_x \times T}$ is a covariate. We allow data with different start and end dates but we assume the data are on a regular grid and padded so that all series have the same length. We use the notation $X^i_{(s,t)} = (X^i_s, X^i_{s+1}, \dots, X^i_{t})$ to denote the covariates from time $s$ to $t$ for unit $i$.  Given a set of covariates $\{X_{(s,t)}^{j}\}_{j=1}^M$, the goal is to predict $Y^i_{t+1}$.

The \setseq layer combines a set network for cross-sectional data processing with an arbitrary sequence-to-sequence layer for modeling temporal dependencies, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:banner}.
\paragraph{\setseq Layer}
Assume that $X^1,\dots,X^M$ are the input units to the \setseq layer, and $Y^1, \dots, Y^M$ are the outputs of the layer. We use temporal chunks as many temporal patterns depend on several time steps to process. 
These inputs are processed in temporal chunks of size $L$, to allow the summary at the current period to use information from recent previous periods, producing feature embeddings via an embedding network, $\phi$. These embeddings are then fed through a set network $\rho$ to produce a permutation invariant, low dimensional, set summary $F_t$
%
\begin{equation}
    \label{linear_set_aggregation}
    F_t = \rho\left( \frac{1}{M} \sum_{i=1}^M \phi\left( X_{(t-L, t)}^{i} \right)  \right) \in \mathbb{R}^r,
\end{equation}
%
We then concatenate the set summary to create an augmented feature $\tilde{X}^i$ that is fed individually into a given sequence layer SeqLayer
\begin{align}
    \tilde{X}^i_t &= \psi([X^i_t, F_t]^T) \\
    {Y}^i_{t+1} &= \operatorname{SeqLayer}(\tilde{X}^i_{(1,t)}),
\end{align}
for all $i \in \{1,\dots M\}$, and $t \in \{1,\dots, T\}$. 
\paragraph{\setseq Model}
The \setseq model replaces a subset of the layers in a sequence model with the \setseq layer. For example, a Transformer, Long Convolution, SSM, or RNN layer can be used for \( \operatorname{SeqLayer} \). 
\paragraph{Alternative Layer Formulations}
We use the linear set aggregation in Equation~\ref{linear_set_aggregation} due to its simplicity and empirically strong performance.
As an alternative, we propose MHA-Seq, a multi-head attention layer that scales quadratically with the number of units. This layer is effective when the cross-sectional dimension is small or when cross-unit dependencies are particularly strong. For each unit, we compute a low dimensional set summary by using multi-head attention, by cross attending to all the other units, by the following equation
\begin{equation}
    F^i_t = \operatorname{MHA}\left([\phi(X^1_{(t-L,t)}), \dots,\phi(X^M_{(t-L,t)}) ]^T\right)_i.
\end{equation}
Other architectures are explored in Appendix~\ref{selection}.
\paragraph{Implementation Details}
\label{setup}

Unless noted, we use LongConv~\cite{fu2023simple} for the sequence layer, two-layer FFNs with dropout for $\phi$, $\rho$, and $\psi$, and chunk size L = 3. MHA-Seq differs only by using five attention heads. Full hyperparameters are in Appendix~\ref{app:exp_details}.

\section{Baselines and Task Selection}
\paragraph{Task Selection}
Common multivariate-forecasting benchmarks—ETTh1, ETTm1, Exchange, and Traffic (see details in \cite{zhou2021informerefficienttransformerlong})—are ill-suited for our study: their unit counts are small enough that a full joint model remains tractable or they lack a clear exchangeable-unit structure. We therefore evaluate on three datasets—a synthetic contagion setting, an equity-portfolio regression task, and a mortgage-risk classification task—each defined over exchangeable units. They contain 1000, 500, and 2500 units with 4, 79, and 52 features per unit, giving cross-sectional dimensions of 4000, 39500, and 130000 that meaningfully stress models built for high-dimensional cross-sections.

\paragraph{General and Domain-Specific Baselines}
\label{baseline_models}
We benchmark against five widely adopted sequence architectures—
Transformer~\cite{vaswani2017attention}, S4~\cite{gu2021efficiently},
H3~\cite{fu2022hungry}, Hyena~\cite{poli2023hyena}, and
LongConv~\cite{fu2023simple}.  
Together they span attention-, state-space-, and convolution-based
methodologies, each of which our \setseq{} layer can augment.  
We treat them as task-agnostic baselines on all three datasets.  
Because these models share a common backbone structure, we hold input/output
dimensions, hidden size, learning-rate schedule, and other
hyperparameters fixed, so the only variation lies in the sequence
layer (and whether a Set module is present).  
This yields a fair comparison across models.  
Each task section also reports results for the strongest
task-specific domain baseline. Hyperparameters for all models are in Appendix~\ref{app:exp_details}.


\section{Synthetic Task}
This section examines the performance of the \setseq model in a setting similar to default risk prediction for corporate and other loans, where contagion has been shown to play an important role~\cite{10.1257/aer.104.8.2527, AZIZPOUR2018154, 10.1257/pol.5.2.313}.  The goal is to predict the transitions probabilities in the next time step for each of the units.
%, when only a subset of the units are observed.  
\paragraph{Task Description}
\label{synthetic_task_desc}
We simulate 1000 exchangeable units, each with binary feature $x \in \{0, 1\}$ over 100 time steps. Units transition between three states (state 3 is absorbing/default) with transition matrix proportional to:
\begin{equation}
\begin{pmatrix}
\label{transition_matrix}
1 + x & 1 & (\lambda_{x,t} + \mu)(1 + 0.1x) \\
1 & 1 + x & (\lambda_{x,t} + \mu)(1 + 0.1x) \\
0 & 0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}
Default intensity evolves as $\lambda_{x,t+1} = \beta \lambda_{x,t} + \alpha N_{x,t}$, where $N_{x,t}$ is the fraction of type-$x$ units defaulting at time $t$. Parameters $\mu = 0.001$, $\alpha = 4$, and $\beta = 0.5$ yield an average default rate of $\sim$1\%. The model captures contagion via $\lambda_{t} = (\lambda_{0,t}, \lambda_{1,t})$, where defaults increase future default risk. 
The core challenge in this synthetic setting is modeling joint dependencies among many units via the low-dimensional latent factor $\lambda_t$, itself a function of each unit’s state.

