\subsection{Generalization Across Number of Input Units}
\paragraph{Task}
Given the setting with $M$ units as described above, the task is to predict the next state transitions accurately based on the previously seen data.
We observe $\bar{N}_t$ which is the fraction of units defaulted when only $n$ out of $M$ units are observed, hence a noisy realization of $N_t$. In the default prediction setting, this correspond to having a large universe of loans evolving jointly, but only observing a limited subset of the data.

\paragraph{Baseline Based on the Kalman Filter}
\label{kalman_section_main}
We develop a strong baseline that represents near ideal performance on the task when trained with infinite data. We assume that the true transition matrix is known, and we also assume that the dynamics of $\lambda_t$, 
$\lambda_{t+1} = \beta \lambda_t + \alpha N_t$ are known, with the only unknown factor being not observing the true fraction of transition to the absorbing state $N_t$, but we only observe
$\bar{N}_t$, where $\bar{N}_t$ is the observed fraction given n out of M units. The problem is now to infer the best estimate of $\lambda_t$ based on partial observations $\bar{N}_1, \dots, \bar{N}_t$. In Appendix~\ref{app:kalman} we derive an estimation $\hat{\lambda}_t$ that is near optimal, based on a Kalman Filter. This estimate is then used along with the true transition dynamics to generate transition probabilities for the next time period for each unit. The goal is hence to achieve performance as close as possible to the Kalman Filter baseline. 

\paragraph{Model Training}
During training we draw the number of units per batch as
$$
D \sim (1 - \gamma)\cdot \delta_M + \gamma \cdot \text{LogUniform}(1, M),$$ with $\gamma=0.08$; Thus most batches use all $M=1000$ units while a few expose smaller pools for generalization. 
An analysis of other sampling schemes along with more training details are provided in Appendix~\ref{app:synthetic_results_detail}.

\paragraph{Metrics}
\label{metrics}
We evaluate models on their ability to predict transitions to the absorbing (rare) state using three metrics: AUC, correlation, and $R^2$. The AUC measures the model’s ability to rank true absorbing transitions above non-absorbing ones. Correlation captures the linear agreement between predicted and true transition probabilities. $R^2$ quantifies the variance explained for the absorbing transitions specifically. See Appendix~\ref{metrics} for formal definitions.



