\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
This paper introduced the Set-Sequence model, an efficient architecture for capturing latent cross-sectional dependencies without manual feature engineering. By learning a shared cross-sectional summary at each period with a Set model and subsequently augmenting individual unit time series for a Sequence model, our approach demonstrates strong empirical performance on synthetic, stock return, and mortgage risk tasks, significantly outperforming benchmarks. While many existing multivariate models are powerful, they are often not natively designed to exploit unit exchangeability in multivariate financial time series. The Set-Sequence model offers a flexible and scalable solution. 

\textbf{Limitations}: Future work should include a deeper exploration of alternative set architectures within the Set model, an investigation into the model's performance on datasets with weaker exchangeability assumptions, and further analysis of the interpretability of the learned set summaries.

