\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

The hole-doped copper oxide (`cuprate’) superconductors exhibit the highest known superconducting critical temperatures ($T_c$) at ambient pressure. A distinctive feature of these materials is the presence of a pseudogap phase at temperatures ($T$) above $T_c$, particularly at low doping levels near the insulating antiferromagnetic state. This proximity suggests a causal relationship between the pseudogap and high-$T_c$ superconductivity. Consequently, elucidating the structure of the pseudogap and the nature of its transition to $d$-wave superconductivity remains a central challenge in the theory of quantum matter.

One influential perspective—the phase fluctuation framework of Emery and Kivelson \cite{EmeryKivelson}—models the pseudogap as a fluctuating superconducting state. In a Born-Oppenheimer approach, a classical XY model captures the thermal fluctuations of the superconducting phase, which modulates pairing amplitude of the Bogoliubov Hamiltonian for quantum electrons. This approach has successfully explained a range of experimental observations \cite{Franz98,Scalapino02,Dagotto05,Berg07,Li_2010,Li11JPhys,Li11PRB,Li_2011,Sumilan17,Majumdar22,YQi23,Xiang24}, including the hallmark `Fermi arc’ features observed in photoemission \cite{Norman98,ShenShen05,Johnson11,Kondo20,Kondo23} and scanning tunneling microscopy (STM) \cite{Hoffman14,Davis14}.

Another perspective is to examine a fluctuating spin density wave (SDW) order parameter (which is a vector in spin space, and so carries spin $S=1$) in the background of a Fermi liquid with a conventional 
Luttinger-volume `large' Fermi surface \cite{SchmalianPines1,SchmalianPines2,Tremblay04,Chubukov23,Chubukov25}. Here too, the focus is on classical thermal fluctuations of the order parameter. This approach yields a convincing theory in regimes where the ground state has long-range SDW order, and has been successfully applied to the electron-doped cuprates \cite{Tremblay04,Chubukov25}. However, there are difficulties in applying the SDW fluctuation theory to the hole-doped cuprates beyond the doping where there is no SDW order at $T=0$ \cite{Chubukov23}. 

An alternative class of theories interprets the `Fermi arcs’ as segments of hole pocket Fermi surfaces, with the back sides exhibiting suppressed spectral weight \cite{SS94,LeeWen96,LeeNagaosaWen,YRZ,YRZreview,Stanescu_2006,Kaul07,Georges08,ACL08,Imada09,Qi10,Punk12,Punk15,Fabrizio22,ZhangSachdev_ancilla,ZhangSachdev_ancilla2,Mascot22,Georges_pseudogap_24,Joshi23}. 
In some approaches \cite{Kaul07,ACL08}, these hole pockets arise from {\it quantum\/} fluctuations of the antiferromagnetic order, which is fractionalized into spinon excitations with spin $S=1/2$ \cite{NRSS89,NRSS90,ChubukovStarykh95,Bonetti2022}, in contrast to the SDW fluctuation theory with $S=1$ paramagnons.
Recent magneto-transport measurements \cite{Ramshaw20,Yamaji24} provide compelling evidence for the existence of such hole pockets in the pseudogap regime—a result difficult to reconcile with the phase fluctuation picture. Notably, the Yamaji effect measurements by Chan {\it et al.} \cite{Yamaji24} in HgBa$_2$CuO$_{4+\delta}$ determine the pocket area based solely on the $c$-axis lattice spacing and the observed Yamaji angle, yielding a fractional area of approximately $1.3\%$ at doping $p = 0.1$.

In this paper, we describe the pseudogap phase above $T_c$ as a `fractionalized Fermi liquid’ (FL*) \cite{TSSSMV03,TSSSMV04,APAV04,Bonderson16,ElseSenthil} of hole pocket Fermi surfaces and a background quantum spin liquid \cite{Kaul07,ACL08,Qi10,Punk12,Punk15,ZhangSachdev_ancilla,ZhangSachdev_ancilla2,Mascot22,Joshi23,Balents25}. The hole pockets were predicted to have area  $p/8$ in a FL* state \cite{Kaul07,ACL08}, yielding $1.25\%$ at $p = 0.1$—in good agreement with the Yamaji effect. Extending the Born-Oppenheimer methodology used in the phase fluctuation theory, we incorporate thermal fluctuations of not only $d$-wave superconductivity, but also additional charge orders. These fluctuations arise from a lattice SU(2) gauge theory \cite{Christos23,Christos24,Luo24,BCS24,ZhangVortex24}, in which superconducting and charge orders are SU(2)-invariant composites of a fundamental Higgs boson $B$ (see Eq.~(\ref{Eq:OPs})). Importantly, this SU(2) gauge symmetry is distinct from global spin rotation symmetry. 

The selection of the SU(2) gauge group and the corresponding lattice gauge theory structure \cite{Christos23,Affleck1988,Affleck-SU2,Fradkin88} is entirely determined by the choice \cite{Christos23} of a critical quantum spin liquid in the FL* theory. Numerical studies of square lattice antiferromagnets \cite{Nahum:2015vka,Becca20,Imada21,Fuzzy24,Meng24,Gu24,Chester24,Sandvik24} provide strong evidence that such a spin liquid is generically stable only across a substantial but finite range of intermediate energy and length scales. Accordingly, we focus our theoretical analysis on the intermediate-temperature pseudogap regime, where the spin liquid description applies, and then invoke confinement at the lowest temperatures. However, we employ a different route to confinement in the doped regime from insulating antiferromagnets.

In the pseudogap regime, we show that thermal fluctuations of $B$ and the link gauge fields $U$ of the SU(2) lattice gauge theory, when coupled to quantum electrons and spinons, transform the FL* hole pockets into the experimentally observed Fermi arcs—analogous to the effect in the phase fluctuation framework. However, in contrast to the latter, the quantum oscillations associated with hole pockets of size $p/8$ are simultaneously present in our theory, aligning with magneto-transport observations \cite{Ramshaw20,Yamaji24}.

Our theory also resolves a notable limitation of the phase and SDW fluctuation pictures. At high temperatures, where superconducting and SDW correlations are weak, their theories predict a large, gapless Fermi surface in the antinodal region \cite{Franz98,Scalapino02,Dagotto05,Berg07,Li_2010,Li11JPhys,Li11PRB,Li_2011,Sumilan17,Majumdar22,YQi23,Xiang24,SchmalianPines1,SchmalianPines2,Tremblay04,Chubukov23,Chubukov25}. This contradicts the experimental phase diagram \cite{phase_diag}, which shows the pseudogap persisting well above the temperature range of significant superconducting fluctuations. In contrast, the FL* theory features Fermi pockets that retain an antinodal gap across all temperatures.

Below $T_c$, we describe the transition from the pseudogap to the $d$-wave superconducting state via a confinement transition of SU(2) gauge fields, driven by Higgs condensation of the $B$ boson. 
There is a long-known connection \cite{Zhang88,KotliarLiu88,IvanovSenthil,LeeNagaosaWen} between spin liquids similar to those we use and $d$-wave superconductivity, but condensation of $B$ on a spin liquid alone leads to nodal quasiparticles with nearly isotropic velocities. We condense $B$ on the FL* pseudogap metal instead, and in this yields \cite{Chatterjee16,Christos24} the observed \cite{Chiao00} highly anisotropic nodal velocities. We also note numerical evidence \cite{Jiang21,Jiang23} showing that $d$-wave superconductivity appears upon doping our spin liquid. 

Here, we present evidence for a Kosterlitz-Thouless transition to a $d$-wave superconductor, characterized by $h/(2e)$ vortices, in our thermal SU(2) lattice gauge theory of $B$ and $U$. Although a $B$ boson carries charge $+e$, confinement leads to the formation of charge $+2e$ gauge-neutral pairs. Remarkably, each vortex core hosts a charge order pattern resembling STM observations by Hoffman {\it et al.} \cite{Hoffman02}.

\subsection{Theory of FL* in single band models}

A defining feature of the FL* metallic phase is its evasion of Oshikawa’s non-perturbative proof of the Luttinger theorem \cite{MO00}, made possible by a background spin liquid hosting fractionalized excitations \cite{TSSSMV03,TSSSMV04}. This construction is relatively straightforward in Kondo lattice models, where conduction electrons couple to localized $f$-spins that can independently form a spin liquid. However, realizing an analogous mechanism in single-band systems is more challenging, as there is no intrinsic separation between electrons responsible for the spin liquid and those forming the Fermi surface.

As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:metals}, a FL* state can nevertheless arise in single-band models via the binding of holons and spinons into composite quasiparticles carrying the same quantum numbers as electrons: charge $\pm e$ and spin $S = 1/2$ \cite{Kaul07,ACL08,Qi10,Punk12,Punk15,Grusdt23,Balents25}. Such a FL* phase was predicted to host four hole pockets, each occupying a fractional area of $p/8$ in the Brillouin zone \cite{TSSSMV03,TSSSMV04,Kaul07,ACL08}, in contrast to the area $p/4$ pockets of thermally fluctuating antiferromagnetic metals in the SDW theory, or of holon metals.
\begin{figure}[!htb]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.45\linewidth]{metals.pdf}
    \caption{Cartoon illustrations and Fermi surfaces of proposed metallic states for the pseudogap phase, obtained by doping a square lattice antiferromagnet with holes of density $p$; following Ref.~\cite{Punk15}. The Fermi surface areas are fractions of the Brillouin zone area, as measured by quantum oscillations. (a) The long-range antiferromagnetic order in the AF metal leads to Luttinger-value Fermi pockets: 2 independent pockets in the magnetic Brillouin zone of $S=1/2$ fermions implies an area of $p/4$. The thermally fluctuating SDW state will also have area $p/4$. (b) The holon metal is a spin liquid with fermionic spinless holons carrying the electrical charge; it has no broken symmetry and 4 independent pockets in the square lattice Brillouin zone of $S=0$ fermions: this leads to a non-Luttinger area of $p/4$. (c) The FL* state has no broken symmetry and 4 independent pockets in the square lattice Brillouin zone of $S=1/2$ fermions: this leads to a non-Luttinger area of $p/8$ \cite{TSSSMV03,TSSSMV04,Kaul07,ACL08,Qi10,Punk12,Punk15,ZhangSachdev_ancilla,ZhangSachdev_ancilla2,Random_ancilla,Mascot22,Joshi23}, in agreement with observations via the Yamaji effect \cite{Yamaji24}.}
    \label{fig:metals}
\end{figure}

We note that the Yang-Rice-Zhang (YRZ) ansatz \cite{YRZ,Johnson11,YRZreview} implies pocket sizes of $p/8$. In this approach, one end of the Fermi surfaces is pinned by the antiferromagnetic Brillouin zone boundary, whereas there is no special role for the antiferromagnetic Brillouin zone in a FL* theory; the YRZ approach also does not have the background of fractionalized excitations, which will be crucial for our analysis here.
We also note that Fermi pockets of size $p/8$ have been discussed in STM \cite{Hoffman14,Davis14} experiments.

%A key consequence of the FL* framework is the requirement of fractionalized spinons to enable non-Luttinger Fermi surfaces. We have recently argued that these spinons provide a natural explanation for features observed in RIXS experiments \cite{BCS24,Sachdev:2025mks,Keimer11,Hayden19}.

Our analysis builds on the ancilla theory of a FL* state in single band models \cite{ZhangSachdev_ancilla,ZhangSachdev_ancilla2,Random_ancilla,Mascot22,Joshi23}. The advantage of this approach is that it leads to a FL* state already at the mean-field level, while maintaining consistency with the non-perturbative anomaly matching arguments on the size of the Fermi surface \cite{MO00,TSSSMV03,TSSSMV04,APAV04,Bonderson16,ElseSenthil}.
As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:ancilla}, this approach maps single-band Hubbard-like models to free fermions in the same band coupled by a Kondo exchange $J_K$ to a bilayer antiferromagnet with rung coupling $J_\perp$.
\begin{figure}[!htb]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.45\linewidth]{ancilla.pdf}
    \caption{The Hilbert space of the ancilla approach consists of a single-band Hubbard-like model and a decoupled bilayer antiferromagnet with rung singlets.
    After a canonical transformation \cite{Random_ancilla,Mascot22}, we obtain the three-layer model in the bottom panel, in which free fermions are Kondo-coupled to the bilayer antiferromagnet.}
    \label{fig:ancilla}
\end{figure}
This mapping can be obtained either ({\it i\/}) via a Schrieffer-Wolff transformation which eliminates the bilayer antiferromagnet \cite{Random_ancilla}, or ({\it ii\/}) via a Hubbard-Stratonovich transformation of the Hubbard interaction by a paramagnon quantum rotor, which is realized by the rung spins of the bilayer antiferromagnet \cite{Mascot22}.
The FL* state is obtained by assuming that $J_K$ dominates after some renormalization, and then the top two layers form a Kondo lattice heavy Fermi liquid, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:flstar}.
\begin{figure}[!htb]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.45\linewidth]{flstar.pdf}
    \caption{The FL* state as realized in the ancilla model. The non-Luttinger Fermi surface of the top two layers is made possible by a fractionalized spin liquid in the bottom layer. Following Ref.~\cite{Christos23}, we take the bottom layer to be a $\pi$-flux spin liquid with an emergent SU(2) gauge field. The Higgs boson $B$ is obtained after a Hubbard-Stratonovich decoupling of $J_\perp$, and it couples the Fermi surface of the top two layers to the spin liquid in the bottom layer.}
    \label{fig:flstar}
\end{figure}
The total density of fermions in the top two layers is then $1+p+1=p~\mbox{(mod 2)}$, and the heavy quasiparticles appear on pockets of fractional 
area $p/8$ \cite{ZhangSachdev_ancilla,Random_ancilla,Mascot22,Joshi23}. A distinguishing feature of the FL* so-obtained is that it also has a spin liquid in the bottom layer, as required by the anomaly matching constraints \cite{MO00,TSSSMV03,TSSSMV04,APAV04,Bonderson16,ElseSenthil}. There is direct experimental evidence for this required spin liquid in the cuprates \cite{BCS24,Sachdev:2025mks,Keimer11,Hayden19}. The ancilla construction of FL* has also been used for numerical studies \cite{Iqbal24,HenryShiwei24,YaHui24} which compare favorably to observations made in cold atomic systems.

The key ingredient for the analysis of our paper is a charge $e$ Higgs boson, $B$, which also carries a fundamental SU(2) gauge charge; a related boson has been considered earlier \cite{Fradkin88,LeeWen96}. As indicated in Fig.~\ref{fig:flstar}, in the ancilla approach, the $B$ boson is obtained by a Hubbard-Stratonovich decoupling of the rung-exchange interaction $J_\perp$, so it couples the heavy Fermi liquid in the upper two layers with the spin liquid in the bottom layer. The FL* pseudogap metal state is obtained when $B$ is uncondensed, while the low temperature superconducting or charge ordered states are obtained when $B$ condenses \cite{Christos23}. As this is a thermal phase transition, we will study the properties of the phases across such a transition by examining the classical thermal fluctuations of $B$ fields and the SU(2) lattice gauge fields $U$ associated with the spin liquid in the bottom layer. These thermal fluctuations are controlled by a lattice energy functional for $B$ and $U$ fields specified in Eqs.~(\ref{Eq:quadraticB}) and (\ref{Eq:quarticB}). 
However, the fermions in all three layers will be fully quantum mechanically accounted for, in the background of static snapshots of thermally fluctuating $B$ and $U$ fields. The fermions are the electrons $c_\alpha$ in the top layer, and the spinons $f_{1\alpha}$ and $f_\alpha$ ($\alpha = \uparrow, \downarrow$) in the bottom two layers. Our method here amounts to a Born-Oppenheimer treatment of the SU(2) gauge theory of Christos {\it et al.\/} \cite{Christos23}, with $B$ and $U$ bosons playing the role of the nuclear positions in molecules \cite{BO27}.

Although we use a gauge-theoretic language of confinement and Higgs condensation to describe the evolution from the pseudogap to the low-$T$ phases, it should be noted that the only precise distinction between low and high $T$ lies in the quasi-long range order of the superconductivity, or other broken symmetries. Nevertheless, the gauge-theoretic description is essential for describing the structure of the fermionic spectrum, as we shall see below.

\subsection{Outline}

We will begin in Section~\ref{sec:BU} by writing down the 2+0 dimensional lattice gauge theory for the SU(2) fundamental, charge $+e$, $B$ scalar fields and the SU(2) lattice gauge fields $U$. This has a form different from the standard lattice gauge theory in the particle theory literature mainly because $B$ experiences a background $\pi$ flux. This fact, along with other symmetry actions of the spin liquid are sufficient to specify the energy functional, which we expand in powers of $B$, keeping terms up to quartic order.

Section~\ref{sec:mean-field} presents the $T=0$ phase diagram of the energy functional obtained in Section~\ref{sec:BU}. As $B$ and $U$ are classical field variables, we have to only minimize the energy. We find states with $d$-wave superconductivity and charge order. In subsequent sections, we work with the parameter values chosen such that the ground state is a $d$-wave superconductor, and the next highest energy minimum has $4 \times 1$ charge order.

Section~\ref{sec:mc} turns to Monte Carlo simulations of the thermal fluctuations of the classical SU(2) lattice gauge theory of $B$ and $U$ fields. We present evidence for a Kosterlitz-Thouless transition to a $d$-wave superconductor driven by vortices with flux $h/(2e)$, with each vortex associated with a charge order halo similar to that observed by Hoffman {\it et al.\/} \cite{Hoffman02}.

Section~\ref{sec:fermions} examines the consequences of the thermal fluctuations of $B$ and $U$ fields on the fermionic sector. The Born-Oppenheimer procedure \cite{BO27} involves diagonalizing the fermion Hamiltonian for each realization of $B$ and $U$ fields, and then averaging the results over the thermal ensemble. The fermions consist of SU(2) gauge neutral electron-like quasiparticles forming small hole pockets (as computed in Ref.~\cite{Mascot22}), and spinons which have 2 independent Dirac nodes and are SU(2) fundamentals. These fermions couple to $B$ fields through a Yukawa term whose form is largely dictated by symmetry. We find that the mixing between the quasiparticles on the pocket backsides and the spinons induced by the thermally fluctuating $B$ fields acts to convert the spectral weight of the fermions into `Fermi arcs' on the front side of the pockets. At the same time, our computation of quantum oscillations in the density of states in presence of a magnetic field shows that the oscillations are not fully quenched upon formation of the arcs. This allows for a compatibility between photoemission \cite{Norman98,ShenShen05,Johnson11,Hoffman14,Davis14,Kondo20,Kondo23} and magneto-transport measurements \cite{Ramshaw20,Yamaji24}.

Appendix~\ref{app:oneloop} includes quantum fluctuations of the $B$ at the one-loop level. The fermion spectral functions are found to be similar to those in Section~\ref{sec:fermions}.

\section{Effective energy functional for $B$ and $U$}
\label{sec:BU}

The structure of effective action functional for $B$ and $U$ fields is fully determined by that of the spin liquid in the bottom layer. Christos {\it et al.} \cite{Christos23,Christos24,Luo24,BCS24} have argued that the $\pi$-flux spin liquid of Affleck and Marston \cite{Affleck1988,Affleck-SU2,Fradkin88} is strongly supported by numerical studies of insulating antiferromagnets \cite{Nahum:2015vka,Becca20,Imada21,Fuzzy24,Meng24,Gu24,Chester24,Sandvik24}, and by observed properties of the pseudogap. This theory is dual \cite{DQCP3} to the $\mathbb{CP}^1$ theory with bosonic spinons \cite{NRSS89,NRSS90}. (On the other hand, the commonly used `staggered-flux' spin liquid \cite{LeeNagaosaWen} is expected to be strongly unstable to single monopole perturbations \cite{Alicea08,Song1}.) We place this spin liquid in the bottom layer, as was indicated in Fig.~\ref{fig:flstar}.
We represent the spins ${\bm S}_{2 \vi}$ on the bottom layer by fermionic spinons $f_{\vi \alpha}$:
\begin{align}
    {\bm S}_{2, \vi} = \frac{1}{2} f_{\vi \alpha}^\dagger {\bm \sigma}_{\alpha\beta}f_{\vi \beta} \,, \label{S2f}
\end{align}
where ${\bm \sigma}$ are the Pauli matrices.
All we need to know about the ${\bm S}_2$ spin liquid are the projective symmetry transformations of the spinons $f_{\vi \alpha}$, and of their coupling to the lattice SU(2) gauge fields $U_{\vi\vj}$. As indicated in Fig.~\ref{fig:ancilla}, the boson $B_\vi$ is introduced by a Hubbard-Stratonovich decoupling of the $J_\perp$ exchange interaction between the heavy Fermi liquid in the top two layers, and the ${\bm S}_2$ spin liquid in the bottom layer. The projective transformations of $B$ then follow from those of the $f_{\vi \alpha}$, as their composite is an electron which does not transform projectively. The symmetry transformations are specified in Table~\ref{tab1}. 
