Note the key property
\begin{align}
T_x T_y = - T_y T_x\,, \label{txty}
\end{align}
realizing the $\pi$-flux on both the fermions and the bosons.
The $f_{\vi\alpha}$ spinons couple minimally to the SU(2) fields when placed in the Nambu form
\begin{align}
\psi_{\vi} \equiv \left( \begin{array}{c} f_{\vi\uparrow} \\ f_{\vi\downarrow}^\dagger \end{array} \right)\,.
\label{eq:Nambu1}
\end{align}
As we review below, these spinon properties {\it fully\/} determine the structure of the effective action for the complex lattice doublet $B_{a\vi }$ ($a=1,2$ is a SU(2) gauge index). 

The analysis is clearest upon introducing a matrix notation for the fermions and bosons \cite{Affleck-SU2,Fradkin88}:
\begin{align}
& \mathcal{C}_\vi \equiv \left(
\begin{array}{cc}
c_{\vi \uparrow} & - c_{\vi \downarrow} \\
c_{\vi \downarrow}^\dagger & c_{\vi \uparrow}^\dagger
\end{array}
\right) \quad , \quad
\mathcal{F}_\vi \equiv \left(
\begin{array}{cc}
f_{\vi \uparrow} & - f_{\vi \downarrow} \\
f_{\vi \downarrow}^\dagger & f_{\vi \uparrow}^\dagger
\end{array}
\right) \quad , \nonumber \\
   &~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ \mathcal{B}_\vi \equiv \left( \begin{array}{cc} B_{1\vi} & - B_{2\vi}^\ast \\ B_{2\vi} & B_{1\vi}^\ast \end{array} \right) \,,
\end{align}
and $U_{\vi \vj} = U_{\vj \vi}^\dagger$ is an arbitrary $2 \times 2$ matrix obeying  
$U^\dagger U = 1$ and ${\rm det}(U) = 1$.
Then the SU(2) gauge transformation $V_\vi$ acts as
\begin{align}
\mathcal{C}_\vi \rightarrow \mathcal{C}_\vi \quad & , \quad 
\mathcal{F}_\vi \rightarrow V_\vi \, \mathcal{F}_\vi   \nonumber \\
\mathcal{B}_\vi \rightarrow V_\vi \, \mathcal{B}_\vi \quad & , \quad 
U_{\vi\vj} \rightarrow  V_\vi \, U_{\vi\vj} \, 
V_\vj^{\dagger} \,. \label{eq:gauge}
\end{align}
A global SU(2) spin rotation $\Omega$ on $(c_\uparrow, c_\downarrow)$ acts as
\begin{align}
\mathcal{C}_\vi \rightarrow \mathcal{C}_\vi \, \sigma^z \Omega^T \sigma^z \quad & , \quad 
\mathcal{F}_\vi \rightarrow \mathcal{F}_\vi \, \sigma^z \Omega^T  \sigma^z  \nonumber \\
\mathcal{B}_\vi \rightarrow  \mathcal{B}_\vi \quad & , \quad 
U_{\vi\vj} \rightarrow  U_{\vi\vj}  \,.
\label{eq:spin}
\end{align}
Finally, the U(1) charge conservation symmetry acts as
\begin{align}
\mathcal{C}_\vi \rightarrow  \Theta \,\mathcal{C}_\vi \, \quad & , \quad 
\mathcal{F}_\vi \rightarrow \mathcal{F}_\vi   \nonumber \\
\mathcal{B}_\vi \rightarrow  \mathcal{B}_\vi \, \Theta^\dagger \quad & , \quad 
U_{\vi\vj} \rightarrow  U_{\vi\vj}  \,,
\label{eq:charge}
\end{align}
where 
\begin{align}
    \Theta = \left( \begin{array}{cc}
        e^{i\theta} & 0 \\
         0 & e^{-i \theta}
    \end{array}
    \right)\,.
\end{align}

It is now simple to verify that the operator correspondence between the electrons and the Higgs boson and the spinons 
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{C}_{\vi}^{\vphantom\dagger} \sim \mathcal{B}_\vi^\dagger  \,\mathcal{F}_\vi^{\vphantom\dagger} \,. \label{eq:CBF}
\end{align}
is consistent with the gauge, spin rotation, and charge conservation symmetries above.
In terms of the matrix components, we can write Eq.~(\ref{eq:CBF}) as 
\begin{align}
c_{\vi \alpha}^{\dagger} \sim
B_{1\vi}^{\vphantom\dagger} f_{\vi \alpha}^\dagger + B_{2\vi}^{\vphantom\dagger} \varepsilon_{\alpha\beta}^{\vphantom\dagger} f_{\vi \beta}^{\vphantom\dagger}  \,, \label{cBf}
\end{align}
where $\varepsilon_{\alpha\beta}$ is the unit antisymmetric tensor for spin SU(2).
From Eq.~(\ref{eq:CBF}) we also deduce that
\begin{align}
B_\vi \equiv \left( \begin{array}{c} B_{1\vi} \\ B_{2\vi} \end{array} \right)
\end{align}
couples minimally to $U_{ij}$, and that $B$ also experiences the flux in Eq.~(\ref{txty}).

These gauge and symmetry constraints yield the needed energy functional $\mathcal{E}_2 + \mathcal{E}_4$ for the $B$ and $U$ fields:
\begin{align}\label{Eq:quadraticB}
& \mathcal{E}_2 [B, U] =  (r+2\sqrt{2}w) \sum_\vi B^\dagger_\vi B_\vi -  i w \sum_{\langle \vi \vj\rangle} e_{\vi \vj} \left( B_\vi^\dagger U_{\vi \vj} B_\vj - B_\vj^\dagger U_{\vj \vi} B_i \right) + \kappa \sum_{\square} 
\left[ 1- \frac{1}{2} \mbox{Re} \mbox{Tr} \prod_{\vi\vj \in \square} U_{\vi \vj} \right] \,.
\end{align}
We have the standard Yang-Mills action for the SU(2) gauge field links $U_{\vi\vj}$ whose self-coupling 
is $g$ such that $\kappa=2/g^2$ and the symbol $\square$ denotes the smallest gauge plaquette. The $B$ bosons have the familiar minimal lattice couplings to the SU(2) gauge field $U_{\vi\vj}$. This leads to the novel and important feature that the nearest-neighbor boson hopping is purely {\it imaginary}, $i w$. We can understand the imaginary hopping by the structure of the fermionic spinon hopping, as discussed below in Eq.~(\ref{eq:fermionhop}). The $B$ bosons also experience the $\pi$-flux via the fixed field $e_{\vi\vj} =- e_{\vj \vi}$:
\begin{align}
 e_{\vi,\vi+\hat{{\bm x}}}  =  1 \,,\quad
 e_{\vi,\vi+\hat{{\bm y}}}  =  (-1)^{x}      \,, \label{su2ansatz}
\end{align}
where $\vi \equiv (x,y)$, $\hat{\bm x} \equiv (1,0)$, $\hat{\bm y} \equiv (0,1)$.

When $U_{\vi\vj} = 1$, we can diagonalize $\mathcal{E}_2[B,1]$
and obtain the bosonic spectrum
\begin{align}
\varepsilon_B ({\bm k}) = r + 2\sqrt{2} w \pm 2w\left(\sin^2 (k_x) + \sin^2 (k_y) \right)^{1/2}\,. \label{eq:bosondisp}
\end{align}
The $2\sqrt{2} w$ was added to the leading term in Eq.~(\ref{Eq:quadraticB}) so that the minimum of the $B$ dispersion is $r$, and the mean-field transition point for $B$ to condense is $r=0$. Note that the minimum is realized at 2 momenta $(\pi/2, \pm \pi/2)$ in the (reduced) Brillouin zone, and this degeneracy enables the multiple competing orders in the Higgs phase \cite{Christos23}.

We also need a quartic potential to stabilize $B$ in the condensed phase:
\begin{equation}\label{Eq:quarticB}
\begin{split}
& \mathcal{E}_4 [B, U] = \frac{u}{2} \sum_{\vi} \rho_{\vi}^2   + V_1 \sum_{\vi} \rho_{\vi} \left( \rho_{\vi + \hat{\bm x}} + \rho_{\vi + \hat{\bm y}} \right) 
 + g \sum_{\langle \vi \vj \rangle} \left| \Delta_{\vi\vj} \right|^2 
 + J_1 \sum_{\langle \vi \vj \rangle}  Q_{\vi\vj}^2 + K_1 \sum_{\langle \vi \vj \rangle}  J_{\vi\vj}^2 \\
&~~~~~~~~~+ V_{11} \sum_{\vi} \rho_{\vi} \left( \rho_{\vi + \hat{\bm x}+ \hat{\bm y}} + \rho_{\vi +\hat{\bm x}- \hat{\bm y}} \right)
+ V_{22} \sum_{\vi} \rho_{\vi} \left( \rho_{\vi + 2\hat{\bm x}+ 2\hat{\bm y}} + \rho_{\vi + 2\hat{\bm x}- 2\hat{\bm y}} \right) \,.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
We have written the quartic potential in terms of SU(2) gauge-invariant bilinears of $B$ with the following physical interpretations which can be deduced directly from the transformations in Table~\ref{tab1}:
\begin{equation}\label{Eq:OPs}
\begin{split}
&\mbox{site charge density:~}\left\langle c_{\vi \alpha}^\dagger c_{\vi \alpha}^{\vphantom\dagger} \right\rangle \sim \rho_{\vi} = B^\dagger_\vi B_\vi^{\vphantom\dagger}\,,  \\
&\mbox{bond density:~} \left\langle c_{\vi \alpha}^\dagger c_{\vj \alpha}^{\vphantom\dagger} + c_{\vj \alpha}^\dagger c_{\vi \alpha}^{\vphantom\dagger} \right\rangle~\sim Q_{\vi \vj} = Q_{\vj\vi} = \mbox{Im} \left(B^\dagger_\vi e_{\vi \vj}^{\vphantom\dagger} U_{\vi\vj}^{\vphantom\dagger} B_\vj \right)\,,  \\
&\mbox{bond current:~} i\left\langle c_{\vi \alpha}^\dagger c_{\vj \alpha}^{\vphantom\dagger} - c_{\vj \alpha}^\dagger c_{\vi \alpha}^{\vphantom\dagger} \right\rangle \sim J_{\vi \vj} = - J_{\vj\vi} =  \mbox{Re} \left( B^\dagger_\vi e_{\vi \vj}^{\vphantom\dagger} U_{\vi \vj}^{\vphantom\dagger} B_\vj^{\vphantom\dagger} \right)\,, \\
&\mbox{Pairing:~} \left\langle \varepsilon_{\alpha\beta} c_{\vi \alpha} c_{\vj \beta} \right\rangle \sim \Delta_{\vi \vj} = \Delta_{\vj \vi} = \varepsilon_{ab} B_{a\vi} e_{\vi \vj} U_{\vi \vj} B_{b\vj}\,. 
\end{split}
\end{equation}
In absence of the quartic potential for $B$, all the above orderings can appear in possible, equivalent Higgs phases of $B$. The coupling constants in the quartic potential will select among these Higgs phases. We choose the quartic couplings in Section~\ref{sec:mean-field} such that the global ground state is a $d$-wave superconductor, and next preferred state is a period-4 charge density wave (CDW) state. Many other choices are possible, and can lead to states with pair density waves and other co-existing orders  \cite{Christos23}. The fact that all these orderings are only selected at quartic order, and democratically treated at leading quadratic order, is an attractive feature of the method of order parameter fractionalization \cite{CSS93}: it provides a rationale for the near-degeneracy of numerous orders observed in various numerical studies.

In principle, all the couplings in $\mathcal{E}_2 + \mathcal{E}_4$ can be derived from a more microscopic theory, when they are expected to be temperature dependent. The microscopic theory of the ancilla model contains fermions coupled to the Higgs boson $B$ via the Yukawa couplings in Eq.~(\ref{Yukawa}), and we should view the effective Higgs potential above as derived from integrating out the fermions.
Such a procedure of determining a classical effective potential for bosons, while integrating out quantum fermions, has been followed in some works on fluctuating superconducting order \cite{Dagotto05,Li11PRB}.

