There are period-4 checkerboard modulations far from the vortex core (arising from the choice in Eq.~(\ref{eq:pars2})), which are distorted near the vortex center. The mechanism of the vortex core-induced charge order was discussed in Ref.~\cite{ZhangVortex24}; the vortex core induces SU(2) gauge flux \cite{SSvortex}, and re-orients $B_\vi$ fields to induce a period-4 charge order.

Upon increasing the temperature, we observe a proliferation of the vortices in Fig.~\ref{fig:BondDensPairingPhLowT}. We expect a Kosterlitz-Thouless transition to the normal state, similar to that in a XY model for the phase of the superconducting order parameter.  To test for such a transition, we measure the helicity modulus $\Upsilon$. This is defined by introducing a U(1) gauge field $A_{\vi\vj} = - A_{\vj\vi}$ which acts on the electromagnetic charge of the $B_\vi$ in Eq.~(\ref{eq:charge}), modifying the hopping term in Eq.~(\ref{Eq:quadraticB}) to~\cite{U1_gauge}
\begin{align}
   i w \sum_{\langle \vi \vj\rangle} e_{\vi \vj} \left( B_\vi^\dagger U_{\vi \vj} e^{-i A_{\vi\vj}} B_\vj - B_\vj^\dagger U_{\vj \vi} e^{-i A_{\vj\vi}} B_i \right)\,. \label{BAhop}
\end{align}
We chose
\begin{align}
    A_{\vi, \vi+\hat{{\bm x}}} = \Theta, ~ A_{\vi, \vi+\hat{{\bm y}}} = 0 
\end{align}
and this induces a twist of $L \Theta$ in the boundary conditions for $B_\vi$ around the $x$-loop of the spatial torus. With this twist, we can obtain the helicity modulus by 
\begin{eqnarray}
   4 \Upsilon = \lim_{\Theta \rightarrow 0} \frac{2  \Delta F}{L^2 \Theta^2}~.
   \label{eqn:HelicityModDef}
\end{eqnarray}
The quantity $\Delta F$ represents the difference in the free energy at each value of the inverse temperature 
$\beta=1/T$,
\begin{eqnarray}
 \Delta F = -\frac{1}{\beta} ~\ln ~\Big \langle \mathrm{exp} \left[-\beta~(\mathcal{E}(\Theta) - \mathcal{E}(\Theta=0)) \right] \Big \rangle_{\mathcal{H}(\Theta=0)}~,
 \label{eqn:FreeEnergyDiff}
\end{eqnarray}
($\mathcal{E} = \mathcal{E}_2 + \mathcal{E}_4$). The leading factor 4 in Eq.~(\ref{eqn:HelicityModDef}) is crucial: it 
arises due to the fact that the SU(2) gauge invariant order parameter $\Delta_{\vi,\vj}$ carries charge $2e$, in contrast to the 
charge $e$ carried by $B_\vi$ in Eq.~(\ref{BAhop}).
Note that while calculating $\Delta F$ the ensemble averaging was done with respect to the gauge field configurations 
without twist. The values of the helicity modulus as a function of temperature for different lattice sizes are 
summarized in Fig.~\ref{fig:helicityMod}. 
\begin{figure}[!htb]
    \centering
        \includegraphics[width=0.6\textwidth]{L64_96_128_Y_Pi_2_vs_T.pdf}
           \caption{The data points for the average helicity modulus $\Upsilon$ as a function of temperature for different lattice volumes, connected by lines for visual clarity. The dashed line has a slope of unity and its point of intersection with the data curves gives the temperature corresponding to the Kosterlitz-Thouless transition in Eq.~(\ref{Eq:TKT}).}
    \label{fig:helicityMod}
\end{figure}
We apply the Nelson-Kosterlitz criterion \cite{PhysRevLett.81.5418} for determining 
the transition temperature $T_{KT}$,
\begin{equation}
    \frac{\pi}{2}~\Upsilon(T=T_{KT})=~T_{KT}\,. 
    \label{Eq:TKT}
\end{equation}
Note that the helicity modulus has an indication of a jump around the temperature associated with Eq.~(\ref{Eq:TKT}), supporting the existence of a Kosterlitz-Thouless transition. 
%There are indications of the expected jump in the superfluid stiffness. 
We believe that larger system sizes are required to unambiguously identify the jump. In our simulation, the XY order parameter is a composite of the underlying degrees of freedom, and the fluctuations of the $U_{\vi\vj}$ have to be fully equilibrated to realize a Kosterlitz-Thouless transition of a charge $+2e$ order parameter more precisely. Nevertheless, our observation of $ \pm 2 \pi$ vortices in the charge $2e$ parameter, and the rapid variation of $\Upsilon$ in Fig.~\ref{Eq:TKT} around the dashed line strongly support the presence of a Kosterlitz-Thouless transition.

Similar to the plot in Fig.~\ref{fig:BondDensPairingPhLowT}, we show the variation of the magnitude and phase of the superconducting order parameter and the bond order with temperature, across the transition in Fig.~\ref{fig:MCorders}.

