\subsection{Gaussian Sampling}
\label{sec:Gaussianfermion}

Our interest in the fermion spectra is mainly in the normal state. Instead of the expensive Monte Carlo simulations in Section~\ref{sec:MCfermion}, this section discusses a simpler Gaussian approximation for the fluctuations of $B_\vi$, which we can expect to be a reasonable approximation in the high temperature phase. The Gaussian approximation is equivalent to approximating the energy by $\mathcal{E}_2$ in Eq.~(\ref{Eq:quadraticB}), and suppressing the gauge fields by setting $\kappa=\infty$. In this section we show that the results for the fermionic spectra in the pseudogap phase are similar to
those obtained using $B$ and $U$ field fluctuations from the full Monte Carlo simulations in Section~\ref{sec:MCfermion}. The advantage of Gaussian sampling is that its rapid numerical averaging allows us to apply a uniform electromagnetic field in the large system sizes needed to study quantum oscillations, as we will see in Section~\ref{sec:QO}.
Care must be taken in applying the Gaussian approximation to the physical system: in particular, we should view the $T$-dependence of the physical quantities as arising not only from the explicit $T$ in 
Eq.~(\ref{eq:Z20}), but also from a $T$-dependent renormalization of the `mass' $r$ from all the non-Gaussian terms.

