\subsection{Quantum Oscillations}
\label{sec:QO}

Finally, we study the electronic Fermi surface in the presence of Gaussian fluctuations by computing oscillations of the density of states induced by a magnetic field. To account for a non-zero magnetic field associated with the U(1) charge in Eq.~(\ref{eq:charge}), we introduce a Peierls phase $t_{\vi\vj}\rightarrow t_{\vi\vj}e^{i\frac{e}{\hbar}\int_{\vi}^{\vj}d\bm{s} \bm{A}\cdot d\bm{s}}$ to all hoppings in the first and second layers of Eq.~(\ref{Top2layers_Hamiltonian}) as well as to the boson hopping $w$ as defined in Eq.~(\ref{BAhop}). The fermions moving in $\pi$-flux are still deconfined and are not coupled to the U(1) gauge field. In our calculations, we choose a Landau gauge such that the mean-field Hamiltonian is translationally invariant in the $y$-direction and a system size of $800\times800$. We compute the density of states at the Fermi level of the $c$ electron for varying magnetic field $H$. We use the kernel polynomial method \cite{Wei_e_2006} to compute the density of states at the Fermi level, taking an expansion up to $N=4800$ polynomials and stochastically computing the trace over real space with $M=4$ real space vectors. The density of states as a function of $\frac{1}{H}$ and its Fourier transform are plotted in  Fig.~\ref{fig:GaussianQO}. 
\begin{figure}[!htb]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Osc.pdf}
    \caption{The density of states $\rho$ as a function of the inverse of the magnetic field $1/H$ (a) and the Fourier transform of $\rho$ (b) as a function of frequency normalized by $f_0$, the frequency corresponding to the total Brillouin zone area. Black dashed lines denote the normalized frequency $\frac{f}{f_0}=n\frac{p}{8}$ for $n=1,2,3,...$, corresponding to the expected frequency of small hole pockets with associated area $p/8$. The same parameters were used as in Fig.~\ref{fig:GaussianBosons_beta} and the oscillations were computed averaged over 3 samples per value of $T$.}
    \label{fig:GaussianQO}
\end{figure}
Using the same values of $T$ and $r$ as in Fig.~\ref{fig:GaussianBosons_beta}, we find oscillations with periodicity corresponding to a frequency $f=({p}/{8})f_{0}=0.025f_{0}$ for hole-doping $p=0.2$ where $f_{0}={h}/({ea_0^2})$ is the frequency corresponding to the area of the Brillouin zone. These results suggest that experimental probes which characterize the Fermi surface volume (including the recent experiments which probed the Yamaji effect \cite{Yamaji24}) will still observe a small Fermi surface with volumes as a fraction of the total Brillouin zone ${p}/{8}$, even when the electronic spectral intensity of the backside pocket is diminished by thermal fluctuations and is not visible in ARPES experiments.

