\section{Conclusions}

Understanding the pseudogap regime in hole-doped cuprate superconductors has remained a long-standing open problem, largely due to the complex interplay between quantum and thermal fluctuations \cite{phase_diag}. On the one hand, the electronic spectrum exhibits a gap-like feature near the anti-nodal momenta, $(\pi, 0)$ and $(0, \pi)$, in a regime where no clear symmetry-breaking order parameter has been identified. This strongly suggests the need for a quantum theory of electrons in a non-traditional, possibly fractionalized, state. On the other hand, numerous competing orders—most notably $d$-wave superconductivity and charge stripe order—emerge at low temperatures and exhibit substantial fluctuations over a broad intermediate temperature range \cite{Fradkin10,Hayward:2013jna,Lee14,Nie_15,Fradkin15,Pepin23,Fradkin25}. 
A natural strategy is to compute the electronic spectrum in the presence of these thermally fluctuating orders \cite{EmeryKivelson,Franz98,Scalapino02,Dagotto05,Berg07,Li_2010,Li11JPhys,Li11PRB,Li_2011,Sumilan17,Majumdar22,YQi23,Xiang24,SchmalianPines1,SchmalianPines2,Tremblay04,Chubukov23,Chubukov25}. However, this approach falls short, as the pseudogap persists at temperatures well above those at which such fluctuations have been experimentally observed.

In this paper, we make significant progress toward resolving this puzzle by combining the following key ingredients:
\begin{itemize}
    \item {\it Quantum origin of the pseudogap:} We associate the pseudogap not with any fluctuating order, but with a fractionalized Fermi liquid (FL*) state \cite{TSSSMV03,TSSSMV04,APAV04,Bonderson16,ElseSenthil} which has hole pockets and a background quantum spin liquid with fractionalized $S=1/2$ spinon excitations \cite{Kaul07,ACL08,Qi10,Punk12,Punk15,ZhangSachdev_ancilla,Mascot22,Joshi23,Balents25}. Each hole pocket has fractional area $p/8$, and this differs from the area $p/4$ that would be obtained from classical thermal fluctuations of the SDW state. The FL* state captures the essential quantum character of the pseudogap regime, independent of any proximate ordering.
    \item {\it Thermal fluctuations by fractionalization:} 
    We fractionalize all competing order parameters into a single charge-$e$ boson, $B$, which also carries a fundamental charge under an emergent SU(2) gauge field, $U$, following Refs.~\cite{Christos23,Christos24,Luo24,BCS24,ZhangVortex24}. The SU(2) gauge structure is inherited from the specific spin liquid underlying the FL* state. We then analyze the thermal fluctuations of the competing orders in a classical lattice gauge theory of $B$ and $U$. The thermal fluctuations interact in a crucial way with the quantum theory of the electrons and the fractionalized spinons.
\end{itemize}

This combination of ingredients leads to several important consequences. First, we obtain a consistent description of the $d$-wave superconducting ground state and its thermal transition to the normal state, driven by the proliferation of $h/(2e)$ vortices. Notably, each vortex core is accompanied by a halo of charge order, in agreement with experimental observations \cite{Hoffman02}.

Second, these thermal fluctuations of $B$ and $U$ fields play a central role in shaping the electronic spectrum. While the zero-temperature FL* state yields well-defined hole pockets, incorporating thermal fluctuations leads to the truncation of these pockets into the Fermi arcs observed in photoemission \cite{Norman98,ShenShen05,Johnson11,Kondo20,Kondo23} and STM \cite{Hoffman14,Davis14} experiments.

Finally, our theory aligns with recent magneto-transport evidence for hole pockets in the pseudogap phase \cite{Ramshaw20,Yamaji24}. In particular, the Yamaji effect measurements of Chan {\it et al.\/} \cite{Yamaji24} reveal a hole pocket of fractional area $p/8$, in agreement with FL* predictions \cite{Kaul07,ACL08}. Crucially, we show that quantum oscillations of pockets of size $p/8$ survive even in the presence of strong thermal fluctuations of $B$ and $U$—fluctuations strong enough to produce Fermi arcs—thereby connecting the seemingly contradictory observations of Fermi arcs and the Yamaji effect within a unified theoretical framework.

