Euclid's \textit{Elements} is not only a monumental work in the history of mathematics but also a source for basic mathematics being learned these days. 
Especially, the existence of the terminology ``Euclidean geometry'' indicates the influence and the pervasiveness of geometric ideas taught by \textit{Elements}. 
Moreover, tools like the Cartesian coordinate system (i.e. analytic geometry) or the axiomatic approach established by Hilbert \cite{Grundlagen} enabled reinterpretation with relatively modern perspectives and therefore a new way of understanding of \textit{Elements}.

Whereas the lesson of \textit{Elements} is widely spread, the way Euclid described it seems less known and of less interest. 
When saying of the Pythagorean theorem, instead of ``$a^2=b^2 + c^2$ in a right triangle,'' how many people would recall \textit{Elements} I.47 ``squares on each of the sides containing the right angle are equal to the square on the hypotenuse?'' 
The difference between both sides may seem marginal at a glance, but it is clear that modern interpretation does not fully address the nuance of the original text.

As another example, let us consider what translation we can give to the following ancient Greek style statement (see Figure \ref{fig:abcd}).
\begin{quote}
Quadrilateral $ABCD$ is equal to the sum of triangle $ABC$ and triangle $CDA$.\footnote{%
In \textit{Elements}, two geometric figures can be equal(\textgreek{ἴσος}) not only in case they are congruent to each other but also if the equality is implied from another equality by \textit{Common Notions} 1-3 in \textit{Elements} I.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[CN1.] Things equal to the same thing are equal to each other.
\item[CN2.] If equal things are added to equal things, then the wholes are equal.
\item[CN3.] If equal things are removed from equal things then the remainders are equal.
\end{enumerate}
More detailed discussion on the meaning of equality in \textit{Elements} can be found in \cite{DeRisi2021}.}
\end{quote}
We may try the lens of analytic geometry, which identifies a polygon as a closed subset of the plane bordered by straight line segments, to translate the above statement. 
However, in this case, the sum of $ABC$ and $CDA$ is not equal to but greater than $ABCD$ by the diagonal $AC$. 
(The equality also fails for similar reasons if polygons are identified as open subsets.)
The conventional perspective that regards a geometric figure as a set of points cannot perfectly translate similar statements.\footnote{Caratheodory \cite{Caratheodory1963} also pointed out this issue and his solution is what we are familiar with: the additivity does not hold for the sets representing the figures but for their contents(areas).}
In other words, this discrepancy indicates that the formulation in analytic geometry corresponds to, but misses some ideas, of the geometric concepts of \textit{Elements}.

On the other hand, another model of Euclidean geometry would be desirable if it could treat the addition of geometric figures as \textit{disjoint} union of sets. 
In the model, for example, the set corresponding to quadrilateral $ABCD$ will be the disjoint union of two sets corresponding to triangles $ABC$ and $CDA$, respectively. 
In that case, we do not need to insert the redundant phrase ``up to boundary'' when reading the statements including the addition or subtraction of geometric figures. 
In addition, the propositions of \textit{Elements} can be understood without substituting the geometric figures by their content(length, area and volume) and not as ``geometric algebra'' as once misunderstood by some scholars.\footnote{For many years there had been debate on the perception of ancient Greek geometry as geometric algebra. 
A brief explanation of this topic can be found in \cite{GrattanGuinness1996} and the most ferocious articles at that time in \cite{Christianidis2004ed}.
} 
That is, the new model may describe the geometric figures in a way closer to Euclid's perspective.

This article will suggest such a model, giving an affirmative answer to the following naturally arising question while reading \textit{Elements}. %
\begin{que}\label{que:1}
Is it possible to reformulate geometric figures of Euclid's \textit{Elements} as subsets of a certain superset, so that the addition between figures corresponds to disjoint union of sets?
\end{que}%
The new formulation, which we call ``refined formulation,'' is based on analytic geometry and augmented with modification that solves the boundary issues mentioned above. 

The remaining part of this article is structured as follows. 
In Section \ref{sec:motiv} the refined formulation is motivated heuristically, through informal languages with emphasis on key ideas. 
Mathematically rigorous explanation is addressed through Sections 3 and 4. 
Whereas Section 3 treats rectilinear figures, the main theorem and its proof, Section 4 includes rectilinear angles and curvilinear figures. 
Finally, in Section 5, the significance of the new formulation is evaluated via reviewing examples from \textit{Elements}.

\section{Motivation} \label{sec:motiv}
Let us call by \textit{conventional formulation} the contemporary coordinate geometry, i.e. the usage of the coordinate system for the representation of geometric figures and relations or operations between them. 
For example, conventional formulation perceives the whole space as a set of tuples of numbers, a straight line in a plane as the solution set of a first-order equation, and a triangle in a plane as the solution set of a system of first-order inequalities. 

On the contrary, this section addresses heuristic arguments toward \textit{refined formulation}, the model which affirmatively answers Question \ref{que:1}. 
Let us denote, when $P$ refers to a geometric figure(line, polygon, polyhedron, etc.) in the conventional formulation, the corresponding entity in the refined formulation by $\sto{P}$. 

\textit{It should be noted that the arguments below may not be mathematically rigorous.}

