Suppose first that a point $C$ lies on a line segment $AB$. 
The line segments $\sto{AC}, \sto{CB}$ and $\sto{AB}$ of the refined formulation satisfy
\begin{equation*}
\sto{AC} \ \sqcup\  \sto{CB} \ =\  \sto{AB}.
\end{equation*}
Since the point $C$ belongs (not necessarily in the set-theoretic sense) to the segment $AB$, it must belong also to $\sto{AC} \sqcup \sto{CB}$. 
Then the point $C$, or the position occupied by $C$, is divisible into two parts, one occupied by $\sto{AC}$ and another by $\sto{CB}$. 
Therefore we expect \textit{any position on the straight line be divisible into two parts} (see Figure \ref{fig:motiv-1d}).

