Next, suppose that a point $D$ is placed inside a triangle $ABC$. 
Again, the triangles $\sto{\triangle ABD},\sto{\triangle BCD},\sto{\triangle CAD}$ and $\sto{\triangle ABC}$ of the refined formulation must satisfy
\begin{equation*}
\sto{\triangle ABD} \ \sqcup\ 
\sto{\triangle BCD} \ \sqcup\ 
\sto{\triangle CAD}\ =\  \sto{\triangle ABC}.
\end{equation*}
Since the point $D$ belongs to the triangle $ABC$, it must belong also to the left-hand side. 
This time, the position represented by the point $D$ is likely to be divisible into three parts, each of which is occupied by one of three triangles $ABD,BCD$ and $CAD$. 
If the occupation is represented by arcs of a circle centered at $D$, then the arcs must behave like the line segments of refined formulation described above. 
It is then reasonable to require \textit{every position on a plane be resolved into a circle of refined formulation}, which is a circle with every position divisible into two parts. (see Figure \ref{fig:motiv-2d}).

Similar arguments for higher dimension yields the following recursive relation: each position on a $d-$dimensional space should be resolved into a set $\stok{\mb{R}}{d-1}$, which satisfies
\begin{equation*}
\stok{\mb{R}}{d-1} = S^{d-1} \times \stok{\mb{R}}{d-2}, \quad \stok{\mb{R}}{0} = \{-1,+1\}
\end{equation*}
where $S^{d-1}$ refers to the $(d-1)-$dimensional unit sphere, and $\stok{\mb{R}}{d-2}$ is the resolution of a poisition in $S^{d-1}$. 
Consequently, \textit{the ambient space of refined formulation must not be merely a set of positions, but the union of occupations over all the positions.}

\section{Rectilinear figures} \label{sec:def}

This section rigorously develops the core components of refined formulation. 
Let us assume the coordinate space under discussion is based on a given field $\bF$, a subfield of $\bR$ which is closed under the operation $x \mapsto \sqrt{1+x^2}$. 
The coordinate space $\bF^d$, where $d$ is a nonnegative integer, will be denoted by $\mb{A}$ or $\mb{V}$, depending on whether regarded as affine of vector space. 
In addition, we assume that $\mb{V}$ is equipped with ordinary dot product $(a_1,\cdots,a_d)\cdot(b_1,\cdots,b_d) = a_1b_1+\cdots a_db_d$ and the norm $\|\cdot \|$ induced by the dot product.

\subsection{Points, lines, polygons and polyhedrons}

In the \textit{conventional formulation}, points, lines, polygons and polyhedrons are identified as specializations of more general notion, \textit{polytope}. 
In this article, the following definitions of polytope and its properties will be adopted. 
%(Readers may refer to [Grunbaum] for different but equivalent definitions.)

A \textbf{closed half-space} of $\mb{A}$ is set of the form $\{\xi \geq 0\} := \{x \in \mb{A}: \xi(x) \geq 0\}$ for a regular (i.e. full-rank) affine functional $\xi:\mb{A} \rightarrow \bF$. 
A \textbf{convex polytope} is the intersection of finitely many closed half-spaces that is bounded, and an unbounded convex polytope is defined similarly but with unboundedness in place of boundedness.
The \textbf{rank} of a convex polytope is the dimension of the minimal affine subspace containing the convex polytope. 
Any convex polytope of rank $k$ is abbreviated to a convex $k-$polytope.
A \textbf{polytope} is the union of finitely many convex polytopes, and the union of finitely many possibly unbounded convex polytopes that is unbounded is called unbounded polytope. 
A $k-$polytope is the union of finitely many convex $k-$polytopes. 
Points, line segments, polygons and polyhedrons, which are the rectilinear figures appearing in \textit{Elements}, are considered $0-,1-,2-$ and $3-$polytopes, respectively. 

The \textit{refined formulation} can be constructed similarly, with modification as follows.
Unless stated otherwise, $k$ is an integer in $\{0,1,\cdots,d\}$. 

\begin{defn}\label{def:sto-basic}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \label{item:def-sto-space} The $(k-1)-$\textbf{resolution} $\stok{\mb{R}}{k-1}$ of $\mb{A}$ and \textbf{refined} $k-$\textbf{space} $\stok{\mb{A}}{k}$ are
\begin{align*}
\stok{\mb{R}}{k-1} 
& =
\begin{cases}
\{(\rho_1,\cdots,\rho_{k}) \in \mb{V}^{k}: \rho_1,\cdots,\rho_{k} \text{ are orthonormal} \}, & k \geq 1 \\
\{ 0 \}, & k = 0
\end{cases}
 \\
%
\stok{\mb{A}}{k}
& = \mb{A} \times \stok{\mb{R}}{k-1}.
\end{align*}
We will denote the entries of an element $\rho \in \stok{\mb{R}}{k-1}$ by $\rho = (\rho_1,\cdots,\rho_k)$,%
\footnote{The $(k-1)-$resolution $\stok{\mb{R}}{k-1}$ can be defined, even if $\bF$ is not closed under the map $x \mapsto \sqrt{1+x^2}$ and $\mb{V}$ is not equipped with inner product, as
\begin{equation*}
\stok{\mb{R}}{k-1}
= {\mathcal{L}^\ast(\bF^k,\mb{V})}/{\sim},
\end{equation*}
where $\mathcal{L}^\ast(\bF^k,\mb{V})$ is the set of all full-rank linear maps $\bF^k \rightarrow \mb{V}$ and the equivalence relation $L_1 \sim L_2$ holds if and only if $L_1 = L_2U$ for some upper triangular matrix $U$. 
It can be proved that this definition is equivalent to the one in Definition \ref{def:sto-basic} under the condition of the main text.
}
and an element of $\stok{\mb{A}}{k}$ by $(x;\rho)$ where $x\in\mb{A}$ and $\rho\in\stok{\mb{R}}{k-1}$. 
The \textbf{closing map} $\kappa^{(k)}$ is the projection $\kappa^{(k)}:\stok{\mb{A}}{k} \rightarrow \mb{A}$
onto the first component. 

\item The $k-$\textbf{refinement} of an affine functional $\xi:\mb{A} \rightarrow \bF$ is the map $\stok{\xi}{k}:\mb{A} \times \mb{V}^k \rightarrow \bF^{1+k}$ defined as
\begin{multline}
\stok{\xi}{k} (x;v_1,\cdots,v_k) = (\xi(x),\ \xi(x+v_1) ,\ \xi(x+v_1+v_2) ,\ \\ \cdots,\ \xi(x+v_1+\cdots+v_k)).
\end{multline}
As in the previous item, a semicolon `;' is placed to distinguish the slots corresponding to $\mb{A}$ and $\mb{V}^k$.

\item A \textbf{refined half-space} is a subset of $\stok{\mb{A}}{d}$ of the form
\begin{equation}
\{\stok{\xi}{d} \succ 0 \} 
:= \{(x;\rho) \in \stok{\mb{A}}{d}: \stok{\xi}{d}(x;\rho_1,\cdots,\rho_k) \succ (0,\cdots,0)\},
\end{equation}
where $\xi:\mb{A} \rightarrow \bF$ is a regular affine functional, and $\succ$ refers to the lexicographical comparison. 
More precisely, the comparison $(a_0,\cdots,a_k) \succ (0,\cdots,0)$ means that there exists an index $i_0$ such that $a_{i_0} > 0$ and $a_i = 0$ for $i < i_0$. 
\end{enumerate}
\end{defn}

The following proposition exposes a peculiar property of refined half-spaces unparalleled by conventional ones.
It states that a refined space $\stok{\mb{A}}{d}$ is ``seamlessly'' divided into two refined half-spaces. 

\begin{lem}\label{lem:half-half}
If $d > 0$, the complement of a refined half-space of $\mb{A}$ is another refined half-space. 
Specifically, we have
\begin{equation*}
\stok{\mb{A}}{d} = \{ \stok{\xi}{d} \succ 0 \} \sqcup \{ \stok{\xi}{d} \prec 0 \}
\end{equation*}
for any regular affine functional $\xi : \mb{A} \rightarrow \bF$.
\end{lem}

\begin{proof}
Let $\xi$ be a nonconstant affine functional. 
The disjointness $\{ \stok{\xi}{d} \succ 0 \} \cap \{ \stok{\xi}{d} \prec 0 \} = \varnothing$ is immediate, and it suffices to prove $\sto{\mb{A}}^{(d)} \subset \{ \stok{\xi}{d} \succ0 \} \cup \{ \stok{\xi}{d} \prec 0 \}$. 
For any $(x;\rho) \in \stok{\mb{A}}{d}$, since the vectors $\rho_1 , \cdots , \rho_d$ are linearly independent, the elements $x,x+\rho_1 , \cdots, x+\rho_1 + \cdots , \rho_d$ of $\mb{A}$ are affinely independent. 
Then we cannot have $\stok{\xi}{d} (x;\rho) = 0$, which means $\xi(x)=\xi(x+\rho_1) = \cdots= \xi(x+\rho_1+\cdots+\rho_d)=0$ and implies $\xi = 0$.
\end{proof}

By tracing the sequence of definitions toward polytopes with refined half-spaces, we obtain novel notions as follows.

\begin{defn}\label{def:sto-poly}
\begin{enumerate}
\item A (possibly unbounded) \textbf{refined convex $d-$polytope} is a subset of $\stok{\mb{A}}{d}$ which is equal to the intersection of finitely many refined half-spaces of $\mb{A}$. 

\item In general, a (possibly undounded) \textbf{refined convex $k-$polytope} in $\mb{A}$ is a subset of $\stok{\mb{A}}{k}$ which is a refined convex $k-$polytope in an affine subspace $\mb{B}$ of dimension $k$ of $\mb{A}$. 
(Note that $\mb{B} \leq \mb{A}$ implies $\stok{\mb{B}}{k} \subset \stok{\mb{A}}{k}$.)

\item A (possibly undounded) \textbf{refined $k-$polytope} in $\mb{A}$ is the union of finitely many refined convex $k-$polytopes in $\mb{A}$. 
\end{enumerate}
\end{defn}

From Definition \ref{def:sto-poly} and Lemma \ref{lem:half-half} we immediately obtain the following property.

\begin{lem}\label{lem:sto-alg}
For any integer $k=0,1,\cdots,d$, the set of (possibly unbounded) refined $k-$polytopes in $\mb{A}$ is closed under union, intersection and relative complement. 
\end{lem}

A hint to the answer to Question \ref{que:1} lies in Lemma \ref{lem:sto-alg}.
Namely, the complement $\sto{R}:=\sto{P} - \sto{Q}$ of a refined polytope $\sto{P}$ in another refined polytope $\sto{Q}$ is again a refined polytope, which means $\sto{P}$ and $\sto{R}$ are disjoint but ``seamlessly'' joined to become $\sto{Q}$. 
This does not hold in general for their images under $\clok{k}$, which are ordinary polytopes.

As illustrated in Section \ref{sec:motiv}, the idea behind refined spaces is to allow geometric figures to occupy a position partially by refining each position in conventional space. 

In the following basic examples, we can observe how such occupation varies on position for a refined polytope.

\begin{ex}\label{ex:poly}
\begin{enumerate}
