\item \label{ex:poly-1} Let $d = \dim\mb{A}=1$. 
Two affine functionals $\xi_1(x) = x-a$ and $\xi_2(x) = -x+b$ $(a,b\in\bF,\ a<b)$ determines the refined line segment
\begin{align*}
\sto{P} 
&= \{\stok{\xi_1}{1} \succ 0\} \cap \{\stok{\xi_2}{1} \succ 0\} \\
&= \{(a;+1)\} \ \sqcup\ \{(x;\pm1):a<x<b\} \ \sqcup\ \{(b; -1)\}.
\end{align*}
In other words, $\sto{P}$ occupies the right half $\{+1\} \subset \stok{\mb{R}}{0}=\{\pm1\}$ of the position $a\in\mb{A}$, the left half $\{-1\}$ of $b \in \mb{A}$ and whole of each $x \in \mb{A}$ for $a<x<b$ (see Figure \ref{fig:ex-dim1}). 
This contrasts the line segment $\kappa^{(1)}(\sto{P}) = [a,b] \subset \mb{A}$ corresponding to $\sto{P}$ in the conventional formulation. 
(The meaning of correspondence will become clear later in Theorem \ref{thm:ref-poly}.)

Let $c$ be between $a$ and $b$, $\xi_3(x) = -x+c$ and $\sto{P}_1=\{\stok{\xi_1}{1},\stok{\xi_3}{1} \succ 0\}$ and $\sto{P}_2 = \{\stok{\xi_3}{1}\prec 0,\stok{\xi_2}{1} \succ 0\}$. 
By direct calculation
\begin{align*}
\sto{P}_1 
&= \{(a;+1)\} \ \sqcup\ \{(x;\pm1):a<x<c\} \ \sqcup\ \{(c; -1)\} \\ 
\sto{P}_2
&= \{(c;+1)\} \ \sqcup\ \{(x;\pm1):c<x<b\} \ \sqcup\ \{(b; -1)\},
\end{align*}
we observe that $\sto{P}_1$ and $\sto{P}_2$ are disjoint and $\sto{P}_1 \sqcup \sto{P}_2 = \sto{P}$.

