\label{fig:abcp}
\end{figure}

Question \ref{que:1} can be associated angles as well: without quantification nor ``up to boundary'', how is it possible to formulate ``the sum of angle $APB$ and angle $BPC$ is equal to angle $APC$'' as disjoint union of sets? (see Figure \ref{fig:abcp})
In some sense, we have already observed the answer, since the construction of refined polytopes in Section 3 contains and parallels that of the current section.%
\footnote{Only rectilinear angles will be discussed in this article. 
Curvilinear angles appear only in one proposition of  \textit{Elements}, in III.16.
}

Recall that $\mb{V}$ is $\bF^d$ as a vector space, equipped with dot product. 
In this article, we define angles to be the spherical version of polytopes as follows.
A \textbf{closed half-space} of $\mb{V}$ is set of the form $\{\varphi \geq 0\} := \{x\in\mb{V}:\varphi(x) \geq 0\}$ for a regular (i.e. full-rank) linear functional $\varphi:\mb{V} \rightarrow \mb{F}$. 
A \textbf{convex angle} is a subset of $\mb{R}=\{v\in\mb{V}:\|v\|=1\}$ which is equal to the intersection of $\mb{R}$ with finitely many half-spaces, and an \textbf{angle} is the union of finitely many angles. 
We define other terms like ``$k-$angle'' similarly to that of polytopes. 
Likewise, this sequence of definitions can be refined as in Section 3.

\begin{defn} \label{def:sto-angle}
\begin{enumerate}
\item The $(k-1)-$\textbf{refinement} of a linear functional $\varphi:\mb{V} \rightarrow \bF$ is the map $\stok{\varphi}{k-1}:\mb{V}^k \rightarrow \bF^{k}$ defined as
\begin{multline*}
\stok{\varphi}{k-1} (v_1,\cdots,v_k) = (\varphi(v_1),\ \varphi(v_1+v_2) ,\ \varphi(v_1+v_2+v_3) ,\ \\ \cdots,\ \varphi(v_1+\cdots+v_k)).
\end{multline*}

\item A \textbf{refined half-space} is a subset of $\stok{\mb{R}}{k-1}$ of the form
\begin{equation*}
\{(v_1,\cdots,v_{k}) \in \stok{\mb{R}}{k-1}: \stok{\varphi}{k-1} (v_1,\cdots,v_{k}) \succ (0,\cdots,0) \}
\end{equation*}
where $\varphi:\mb{V} \rightarrow \bF$ is a regular linear functional, and the meaning of $\succ$ is the same as in item 3 of Definition \ref{def:sto-basic}.

\item A \textbf{refined convex $(d-1)-$angle} is a subset of $\stok{\mb{R}}{d-1}$ which is equal to the intersection of finitely many refined half-spaces of $\stok{\mb{R}}{d-1}$. 
In general, a \textbf{refined convex $(k-1)-$angle} is a subset of $\stok{\mb{R}}{k-1}$ which is a refined convex $(k-1)-$angle in $\stok{\mb{S}}{k-1}$, where $\stok{\mb{S}}{k-1}$ is $k-$resolution of an affine $(k-1)-$subspace $\mb{B}$ of $\mb{A}$.
A \textbf{refined $(k-1)-$}angle is the union of finitely many refined convex $(k-1)-$angles.
\end{enumerate}
\end{defn}

\begin{ex}
Already in item 3 of Example \ref{ex:poly} we encountered refined angles. 
If $\varphi_1,\varphi_2$ and $\varphi_3$ are respective linearizations of $\xi_1,\xi_2$ and $\xi_3$, then the refined angles $\sto{\alpha}_j$ and $\sto{\alpha}_{jl}$ can be represented as
\begin{align*}
\sto{\alpha_j} &= \{\stok{\varphi_j}{2} \succ 0 \} &&(j\in\{1,2,3\})\\
\sto{\alpha_{jl}} &= \{\stok{\varphi_j}{2} \succ 0\} \cap \{ \stok{\varphi_l}{2} \succ 0\} && (j,l \in \{1,2,3\},\ j\neq l).
\end{align*}
And the equation \eqref{eq:ex-angle-ahead}, copied below
\begin{equation*}
\sto{\beta}_3\sqcup\sto{\gamma}_3 = \sto{\alpha}_3, \quad
\sto{\beta}_{12} \sqcup \sto{\gamma}_{12} = \sto{\alpha}_{12},\quad
\sto{\beta}\sqcup\sto{\gamma} = \stok{\mb{R}}{1},
\end{equation*}
answers the question raised with Figure \ref{fig:abcp}.
\end{ex}

By parallel arguments as in Section 3, Theorem \ref{thm:ref-poly} can be established and proved for angles as well, though it will not be repeated here. 
Meanwhile, the heuristic arguments in Section \ref{sec:motiv} can be formalized finally. 

\begin{prop}\label{prop:induct}
Let $P$ be a full-rank $k-$polytope in $\mb{A}$ and 
\begin{equation*}
UP_p 
= \{v \in \mb{V}:\|v\| = 1,\ p + tv \in P \text{ for sufficiently small } t > 0 \}
\end{equation*}
the set of unit tangent vectors of $P$ at $p$.
Then $UP_p$ is also full-rank for each $p \in P$, and we have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:induct}
\sto{P} = \bigsqcup_{p \in P} \{p\} \times \sto{UP_p}
\end{equation}
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
Suppose first that $P$ is a half-space, and let $\xi$ be an affine functional on $\mb{A}$ such that $P = \{{\xi} \geq 0 \}$ and $p$ an element of $P$. 
We have
\begin{equation*}
\sto{UP_p} = 
\begin{cases}
\stok{\mb{R}}{d-1}, &\xi(p) > 0 \\
\{\stok{\varphi}{d-1} \succ 0 \} , & \xi(p) = 0
\end{cases},
\end{equation*}
and equation \eqref{eq:induct} holds in this case. 
For general $P$, the equation follows from Theorem \ref{thm:ref-poly} and its counterpart for angles, especially the items about compatibility with set union and intersection. 
\end{proof}

It should be noted that Proposition \ref{prop:induct} for angles also holds.

\subsection{Toward curvilinear figures}

\textit{Elements} treats not only rectilinear figures, lines, polygons, polyhedra and angles, but also curvilinear figures. 
To formulate the latter consistently, we paraphrase Proposition \ref{prop:induct} as follows: 
if $R \subset \mb{A}$ is a curvilinear figure such that the set of unit tangent vectors
\begin{equation*}
UR_p := \{v \in \mb{V}: \|v \| = 1,\ v = \gamma'(0) \text{ for some smooth curve } \gamma :(-\epsilon,\epsilon) \rightarrow R\}
\end{equation*}
is well-defined and ``nice'' enough, we can define the refined version of $R$ as
\begin{equation} \label{eq:sto-curv}
\sto{R} = \bigsqcup_{p \in R} \{p\} \times \sto{UR}_p.
\end{equation}

This subsection is closed by showing two representative cases, rather than developing equation \ref{eq:sto-curv} into the rigorous definition (including the precise range of $R$ for which $\sto{R}$ is defined).

