\begin{equation*}
\sto{\overline{N}(p,r)} 
= \bigsqcup_{\|x-p\|<r}\{x\}\times \stok{\mb{R}}{d-1} \ \sqcup \ \bigsqcup_{\|x-p\| = r} \{x\} \times \{ \stok{(p-x)^\ast}{d} \succ 0 \}
\end{equation*}
For example, this formulation can explain a circle with inscribing and circumscribing regular polygons, as Archimedes did for the quadrature(calculation of area) of circles (see Figure \ref{fig:circle}).

\item (Hypersphere) The hypersphere $S(p,r)=\{x\in\mb{A}:\|x-p\|=r\}$ centered at $p \in \mb{A}$ with radius $r$ becomes, by equation \eqref{eq:sto-curv},
\begin{equation*}
\sto{S}(p,r) := \{ (p+rv_0; v_1, \cdots, v_{d-1} ) :  v_0,\cdots,v_{d-1}\text{ are orthonormal} \}.
\end{equation*} 
%\item (Cylinder) If $\dim \mb{A}=3$, the infinite (right) cylinder with axis $L \leq \mb{A}$ ($\dim L=1$) and radius $r$ becomes, by equation \eqref{eq:sto-curv},
%\begin{equation*}
%\bigsqcup_{\mathrm{dist}(x,L) <r} \{x\} \times
%\stok{\mb{R}}{2} \ \sqcup\ \bigsqcup_{\mathrm{dist}(x,L)=r} \{x\} \times \{ \stok{{h_{x,L}}^\ast}{3} \succ 0 \}
%\end{equation*}
%where $h_{x,L} \in \mb{V}$ refers to the unit normal vector of the surface of cylinder at $x$. 
%Cylinders with finite height can be obtained by taking intersection with two refined half-spaces.
%
\item (Cone) If $\dim \mb{A}=3$ the infinite (right) cone with apex $p \in \mb{A}$, axis parallel to $u \in \mb{V}$ and the angle between a generator and the axis $\theta$ becomes, by equation \eqref{eq:sto-curv},
\begin{multline*}
\bigsqcup_{\substack{v\cdot u < \cos\theta \\ t > 0}} \{p+tv\} \times \stok{\mb{R}}{2} 
\ \sqcup\ \bigsqcup_{\substack{v\cdot u = \cos\theta \\ t > 0}} \{p+tv\} \times \{ \stok{w_{v,u,\theta}^\ast}{3} \succ 0\} \\ 
\ \sqcup\ \{p\} \times \left( 
\bigsqcup_{\substack{v\cdot u < \cos\theta}} \{v\} \times \stok{\mb{R}}{1} 
\ \sqcup\ 
\bigsqcup_{\substack{v\cdot u = \cos\theta}} \{v\} \times \{ \stok{w_{v,u,\theta}^\ast}{2} \succ 0\}
\right),
\end{multline*}
where $w_{v,u,\theta} = (\cot\theta) v - (\csc\theta) u$ is the vector obtained from rotating $v$ toward $u$ by $90$ degrees.
In this example, the angle at $p$ is a curvilinear angle and required equation \eqref{eq:sto-curv} once more for calculation.
\end{enumerate}
\end{ex}
Although not addressed above, the same can be done for cylinders and cones as well, or even for the regions surrounded by a spiral and radii, or a parabola and a chord appearing in Archimedes' works. 

\section{Re-reading \textit{Elements}}

The refined formulation introduced in this article is especially distinguished from the conventional one, in that each figure is not a collection of points (positions) but of angles. 
Accordingly, figures of different dimensions become subsets of different supersets, $\stok{\mb{A}}{k}$ with varying $k$.
This causes, for example, ``A line segment $\sto{P}$ belongs to a polygon $\sto{Q}$'' different from $\sto{P} \subset \sto{Q}$, and ``A line $\sto{P}$ and a polygon $\sto{Q}$ meets each other at a point'' different from $\sto{P} \cap \sto{Q} \neq \varnothing$.
%\footnote{The term \textit{intersection} literally means ``cut between'' rather than ``common part.''}
This kind of relation must be formulated differently, but it is clearly defined by one-to-one correspondence ensured by Theorem \ref{thm:ref-poly}. 
The details will not be covered in this article.

Meanwhile, the new model fits well with certain characters of ancient Greek mathematics.\footnote{Not in every aspect, though. 
For example, the existence of ambient space is not perceived in ancient Greek mathematics \cite{DeRisi2015ed}.}
Namely, the quantitative concepts of geometric figures like length, area and volume did not exist, and indeed not present in \textit{Elements}. 
Instead, geometric figures themselves were added, subtracted and compared to be equal, greater or less. 
What nowadays is expressed as the product of two lengths was called ``the rectangle contained by two line segments.''\footnote{For more discussion on the usage of geometric concepts of ancient Greek mathematics, see \cite{GrattanGuinness1996}. 
} 
The refined formulation helps readers understand this authentic perspective of ancient Greek Mathematics, especially when facing the following examples from \textit{Elements} (see Figure \ref{fig:elements}).

