\begin{ex} \label{ex:I.35}
\textit{Elements} I.35 reads: 
if two parallelograms $ABCD$ and $EBCF$ are on the same base $BC$ and between the same pair of lines $AF$ and $BC$, then $ABCD$ is equal to the parallelogram $EBCF$. 

The proof proceeds roughly as follows: 
from property of parallelograms triangle $EAB$ is equal to triangle $FDC$, and then by subtracting triangle $DGE$, the common part, quadrilateral $ABGD$ is equal to $EGCF$. 
Adding the same triangle $GBC$ to each, quadrilateral $ABCD$ is equal to $EBCF$. 

Note that removing a region from another and putting regions together can be translated fluently into the language of refined polygons and set union/difference.
\end{ex}

\begin{ex}
\textit{Elements} I.32 reads: 
if a point $D$ is on the extension of side $BC$ of a triangle $ABC$, then the exterior angle $ACD$ is equal to (the sum of) two interior opposite angles $CAB$ and $ABC$, whence the sum of three interior angles $ABC,BCA$ and $CAB$ is equal to two right angles.

The proof proceeds roughly as follows: 
If a line $CE$ is drawn parallel to line $AB$, then the angle $CAB$ is equal to the angle $ACE$, and the angle $ABC$ is equal to the angle $ECD$. 
Therefore the sum of angles $CAB$ and $ABC$ is equal to $ACD$, and by further adding the angle $BCA$ gives two right angles.

In this case, refined angles and set union explain how the sum of angles (not their sizes) can be another angle. 
\end{ex}

\begin{ex}
\textit{Elements} II.1 reads: 
if two line segments $A$ and $BC$ are given, and $BC$ is cut at two arbitrary points $D$ and $E$, then the rectangle contained by $A$ and $BC$ is equal to (the sum of) rectangles contained by $A$ and $BD$, $A$ and $DE$ and $A$ and $EC$. 

The proof is straightforward by comparing the entire rectangle and the sum of the parts. 
While the explanation with refined polygons operates as effectively as in \textit{Elements} I.35, it admits one more advantage. 
The content of \textit{Elements} II was once considered \textit{geometric algebra}, including \textit{Elements} II.1 as algebraic statement $x(y+z+w) = xy+xz+xw$ (say, $x,y,z,w\in \bF$) in geometric guise. 
Currently, such a view is considered not to represent the philosophy of ancient Greek mathematics.
The refined model provides a modern, non-algebraic but geometric perspective toward \textit{Elements} II, as exemplified above.
\end{ex}

As the last example, we see how Wallace–Bolyai–Gerwien theorem is formulated for refined polygons. 
Originally this theorem states that two polygons with the same area can be transformed into one another by finitely many times of cut-and-paste. 
In the refined version, two polygons are indeed partitioned, i.e., expressed as disjoint union without exception on boundaries, into pieces.

\begin{thm}[Wallace-Bolyai-Gerwien]
Two polygons $P$ and $Q$ of 2-dimensional space $\mb{A}$ has the same area if and only if $\sto{P}$ and $\sto{Q}$ are equidecomposable, i.e., there exist partitions
\begin{equation*}
\sto{P} = \bigsqcup_{i=1}^m \sto{P}_i,\quad
\sto{Q} = \bigsqcup_{i=1}^m \sto{Q}_i
\end{equation*}
of $\sto{P}$ and $\sto{Q}$ such that $P_i$ is congruent to $Q_i$.
\end{thm}

