\begin{proof}[Sketch of proof]
Well-known proofs (see, e.g., \cite[Theorem 24.7]{Hartshorne2000}) work, and a variant is provided below (see Figure \ref{fig:WBG}).

The equiareal transform of \textit{Elements} I.35 (see Example \ref{ex:I.35}) can be realized by cut-and-paste.
This completes the proof if both $\sto{P}$ and $\sto{Q}$ are parallelograms because any two parallelograms are interchangeable via applying equiareal transform twice. 
If both $\sto{P}$ and $\sto{Q}$ are triangles, each can be reassembled into a parallelogram, recurring to an already proven case. 
In general cases, $\sto{P}$ and $\sto{Q}$ can be decomposed into the same number of triangles with matching areas, and for each pair the proven case applies. 

Note that in the statements above decomposition always means disjoint union, not merely up to boundary.
\end{proof}

