\p
It suffices to show $E(\h ij)_n^{s,s}=0$ for $s>0$ by Lemma \ref{HS}.
By degree reason, $E(\h ij)_n^{s,s}=0$ unless $q\mid s$.
For $s=kq$, $E(\h ij)_n^{kq,kq}=0$ if $k\ge 2$ or $k=1$ and $n^2< 2p-2=q$.
If $n^2=q$ (resp. $=q+1$), then  by \kko{isoD}, $E(\h ij)_n^{q,q}\cong E(\h ij)_n^{0,-q}=0$ (resp. $E(\h ij)_n^{q,q}\cong E(\h ij)_n^{1,-q}=0$).
\q
\fi

