\lem{E2ex}{Suppose \kko{cond}. % that $n^2\le 2p-1$ and $n\le p-2$.
Then, $E_2^{s+q, tq}(W_n)_n= 0$ unless 
\begin{itemize}
\item $(s,n^2)=(1,q+1)$ or $(0,q)$, and $t\cg 0$  $(e(n))$.
\item $(s,n^2)=(0,q+1)$, and $t\cg -p^j$  $(e(n))$ for $0\le j<n$.
\end{itemize}
}

\p
By \kko{E2W} and Lemma \ref{HS}, it suffices to show $H^{s+q, tq}E(\h ij)_n=0$ unless the conditions in the lemma.

If $s\ge 2$, then $s+q\ge 2p>n^2$, and so $H^{s+q, tq}E(\h ij)_n=0$.
Suppose that $s=1$ and $n^2\le q$, or that $s=0$ and $n^2\le q-1$. Then $q+s>n^2$, and $H^{s+q, tq}E(\h ij)_n=0$.
For $(s,n^2)=(1,q+1)$ or $(0,q)$, $H^{q+s,*}E(\h ij)_n\cong \Z/p\lrk{g_n}$. Since $|g_n|\cg 0$ mod $2(p^n-1)$, the lemma holds in this case.
For $(s,n^2)=(0, q+1)$, $E(\h ij)_n^{q,tq}\cong \Z/p\lrk{\h ij^*}$.
Since $d((\h 1j\h{i-1}{1+j})^*)=\pm \h ij^*$, we see $\h ij^* =0\in H^{q,tq}E(\h ij)_n$ unless $i=1$.
It follows that $H^{q,-p^jq}E(\h ij)_n\cong \Z/p\lrk{\h 1j^*}$.
\q

