%Gao et al. [] recently proposed Local Structure-Aware Anisotropic Convolution LSA-Conv for 3D mesh reconstruction to handle irregular 3D mesh data by learning adaptive filters based on local geometry. Unlike isotropic methods, LSA-Conv uses anisotropic filtering that adjusts weights dynamically through adaptive weighting matrices, similar to attention mechanisms, to capture directional dependencies. To improve efficiency, matrix factorization reduces the parameter size, and residual connections further enhance reconstruction accuracy. However, LSA-Conv has limitations. Its focus on local geometry may miss critical global topology, leading to suboptimal results on complex 3D shapes. Additionally, while matrix factorization improves efficiency, dense meshes still demand high computational resources. This makes LSA-Conv less efficient compared to methods like FEASTConv, which better balance both local and global mesh structures.

In this paper, we propose 3DGeoMeshNet, a novel GCN-based framework designed to overcome the limitations of both spectral and spatial approaches. Our network uses anisotropic convolution layers that operate directly in the spatial domain, effectively capturing both global and local features without the need to convert the data into intermediate formats like voxel grids or point clouds. Our multi-scale encoder-decoder architecture features separate global and local pathways, allowing for efficient feature extraction and high-fidelity mesh reconstruction across varying scales.

